insert into public.chunks (id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length)
select id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length
from jsonb_to_recordset($jsonpayload$
[{"id": "ed95f7de-3787-4256-97bb-9c0f9c7babab", "episode_id": "6104b1ba-29fe-43f4-9df8-64c589412ee9", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488b", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:06:05): Yeah. Wow. Well, Megan, I want to thank you for your time. This has been a lot of fun, and I feel like I've walked away from this conversation with, well, I've learned a lot, but also I have way more questions than I did going into this, so thank you. I appreciate it.\n\nMegan Averell (01:06:20): Of course. And those are great, maybe potential future research questions, right? There's always a question that's a testable proposition in my world, so potentially those are questions you could go ask a researcher to get them know how to solve it for your brand.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:06:36): Yeah. Well folks, I highly, again, recommend you go check out Megan's LinkedIn. She has another post that I really appreciated. I think the title of it was Your Brand might be The Gas Station with Good Ice. I won't explain what that's about, but go check it out. You won't be disappointed. Megan, where can folks follow along with you or learn more about the insight in?\n\nMegan Averell (01:06:59): Yeah, so I'm on Twitter at megatron with two Gs, and as Cole mentioned, I've been doing a little series of articles on LinkedIn that are public and easily searchable called Research Fallacy Fridays, and my website is just www.theinsightinwithtwons.com. So there's a contact us form. If any of these questions have brought up anything that as you're trying to puzzle over in your organization, I would love to help you solve them.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:07:28): Awesome. Well, Megan, thank you again. I hope you have a great rest of your day. And who knows, maybe next time we chat you'll have fully adopted rollerblading and it'll become your next thing.\n\nMegan Averell (01:07:40): Who knows, right? Post pandemic. I'm not rolling anything out.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:07:44): Alright, well take care.\n\nMegan Averell (01:07:45): Thanks.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:07:46): See you. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 522, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Megan Averell"], "start_timestamp": "01:06:05", "end_timestamp": "01:07:46", "podcast_index": 101, "episode_name": "Ep. 101: Why Consumer Research is so Important | Megan Averell | The Insight Inn", "slug": "ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-101-why-consumer-research-is-so-important-megan-averell-the-insight-inn", "guest_name": "Megan Averell", "guest_title": "Founder at the Insights Inn", "categories": ["brand", "strategy"], "short_description": "Megan Averell is the founder of the Insight Inn, a qualitative research and strategy firm, to bring people back into the heart of business decision-making.", "podcast_length": "1:08:00"}, {"id": "8a1f9b8d-7332-4852-96ea-59d3050f424e", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Bethany Evans (00:00:02): Whether it's blockchain, crypto, and wallets, NFTs, those are all, I think, building blocks that you would apply to the Metaverse to create the brand experience that you want. Whether that's roadblocks or some of these more sophisticated executions, we're starting to see that. Yeah, it's, it's so much newness all at once. There's always that element of when anything new comes out. Yeah, it's scary. Sometimes you just want to bury your head in the sand and be like, I cannot learn. One more thing.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Hallborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Holborn, and today I'm sitting down with Bethany Evans. She is the Senior Director of marketing over at the North Face. Bethany, how are you?\n\nBethany Evans (00:01:16): Good, Cole. Thanks for having me.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:18): Yeah, thanks for joining us today. How's your day going?\n\nBethany Evans (00:01:21): It's good. It's a Monday. I had soccer with my kids all weekend, so just getting back in the swing. What about you?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:28): Yeah, it's been a good day. I've actually been recording a couple podcast episodes today, so I feel like I'm just in total podcast mode and I've been learning lots, and so I'm excited to have this conversation with you today.\n\nBethany Evans (00:01:41): You're in the zone. I love it.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:01:44): How old are your kids? And I guess spring soccer probably just started up, didn't it? Or has been going on for a little bit?", "token_estimate": 520, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:00:02", "end_timestamp": "00:01:44", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "8caae4da-4a2a-4c79-98d7-b9942a1bbe12", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Bethany Evans (00:01:49): Yes, first weekend and luckily No Snow, which you never know in Colorado in the spring. But yeah, I have two boys. They're eight and 12, and the youngest is super into soccer, so it's really fun.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:02:02): Are you guys one of those families where you post up for the whole Saturday at the soccer field and you've got the whole setup? It just rolls right out of the car and got it pretty dialed\n\nBethany Evans (00:02:12): Sometimes. We also bring our dogs, they love running around, so yeah, it's quite the production between kids, dogs, coolers, chairs, extended family. It's so fun though. It's great.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:02:27): Nice. Well, I can't help but notice your Zoom background here. You've got this tagline we were just talking about a little bit earlier, discover Your Trail. I dunno if that's something you can quickly tease us a little bit about, but where's the inspiration for that come from?\n\nBethany Evans (00:02:42): Yeah, great question. It's our new Spring campaign that just launched. We usually do a spring campaign and a fall campaign, and this one was really around, we've been doing more and more with trail running. We launched our new vector line of shoes about this time last year, and so we have some athletes that are trail runners and for the people like myself who are not good runners, you can always walk on a trail or just go hiking, but the idea is just about discover your trail. Being the trail means so many things to different people. It could literally just be a trail in your neighborhood or you might want to use all trails and go find something a little bit more intense or a little farther out. But I think so many people over the pandemic re-embraced the idea of getting outside, and that was a haven for many of us when we couldn't go anywhere else. So great. Let's keep that going. Yeah, so that's the campaign.", "token_estimate": 476, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:01:49", "end_timestamp": "00:02:42", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "682010f9-9029-40af-9142-55a260e401b0", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:03:38): So speaking of discovery, Bethany, we're going to be talking all about emerging technologies and how brands and how people are kind of reacting and analyzing and trying to understand these various tools that are coming out. It seems like every day, I don't know about you, but I feel like some of the big ones are like the Metaverse, NFTs, crypto. There's a whole bunch of new stuff that's been coming out recently, and some of it seems like it's gaining a lot of steam, and I got to imagine from a brand's perspective, you guys are constantly asking yourselves, what do we pay attention to? What do we not pay attention to? So that's kind of what we're going to be jumping into a little bit today, but I'd love if you could first give us a breakdown of your job, your job description, and what your background is.", "token_estimate": 210, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:03:38", "end_timestamp": "00:03:38", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "b2c8592a-f627-4406-94a3-18275df72e29", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Bethany Evans (00:04:22): Yeah, I'd be happy to. It is interesting because I did not go to school for marketing. I went to school for journalism, and when I started out of school I was working in different startups, doing some operations, some marketing, wearing a lot of different hats as you do in the startup world. I worked for a company called Buy With Me, which was one of the original players in the social selling space. So right when living social and Groupon we're getting big, you probably remember a decade back Buy With Me was one of the players in that space and full credit to a man I worked with named Matt. He realized the power of starting to literally taking what's in the name, social, social selling, great, take it to social. So as we were working on daily deals, we would go onto Twitter at his suggestion and if people were tweeting, oh, my back hurts, or I need a massage maybe in Chicago, and we had a daily deal for a half off massage in Chicago, we would respond on Twitter from the Buy with Me Twitter handle and say, guess what? Check out this deal. Just very, very early social listening and responding, and I got laid off. The company was bought by Gil and they were really buying the customer list. So myself and pretty much the whole company got laid off. And so anyone listening who has been laid off or is maybe dealing with that now definitely can sometimes take you in a really positive direction. For me, I had been so go, go, go. Ever since I got out of school. (00:06:01): My oldest was a baby at the time, and so I had just been so busy and I hadn't really had a chance to reflect on, wait, what am I doing with my career? At the time I was in my twenties, you all have that quarter life crisis, what am I doing? And I realized when I was laid off, sitting at home watching a lot of tv, oh, I liked that social thing. I think there might be something there. And this was back when very few people were working full-time in social. It wasn't really considered a career yet, or it was just starting to be. So this will definitely kind of speak to what we're getting to with the metaverse and all of these giant shifts that we're seeing right now. But 10, 15 years ago, the big shift was to social. Social was new. Facebook had been this college tool, and people were just starting to realize, wait, you could do more with it. (00:06:55): There's this thing called Twitter, this thing called Tumblr. So I was very fortunate I was able to find a job starting social media and also doing some PR and other marketing for a company, a hotel company in town in Dallas where I was living at the time. And very quickly got recruited by JC Penney. They had been outsourcing all of their social media to agencies as most companies were at that time. I think going back to what you said at the beginning of this, companies trying to figure out, no one knew what to do with social. It was like, oh, let's just have the agency handle that tiny bit of their scope on the side. And as it was clearly becoming more important to have a team internally who could quickly react to social conversation and even start to solve customer questions and issues on social, there was this big movement at the time to insource social and build out in-house teams. (00:07:56): So I went over to JC Penney to help grow their social presence, both on the paid and organic side and led the community team as well. So on Black Friday, I was tweeting at 2:00 AM along with many of my teammates. Very humbling experience, very good experience. Yeah, so that is kind of how I got into marketing was through social media, and eventually I went to Southwest Airlines and then to the North Face, and I have been able to grow and learn different areas of marketing over the years. So currently I oversee our paid media, our owned channels, like the app and SMS and email and our loyalty program. So I'm mainly working on our North America D two C business, supporting that through these channels. And yes, it feels like we're undergoing, as you said, another seismic shift right now where we're kind of at this turning point where all of these emerging technologies, Web3 0.0, NFTs, metaverse, all of this lingo that's being thrown out really I would say over just the last year, it feels like it has escalated and it presents so many opportunities, but also many risks. And I think that's what everyone from a brand perspective is trying to figure out right now is do you be a first mover? Do you wait and see how things shake out? What's the risk versus reward? For my business specifically? Yeah, it's a lot to contemplate.", "token_estimate": 1157, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:04:22", "end_timestamp": "00:04:22", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "bc797364-67ce-4a59-83a1-cee74134c239", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:09:31): Are you someone, we talk about these things? I don't know what your reaction is, but usually my reaction is like, oh, this is too complicated. I don't understand it, therefore I don't want to understand it, which I realize is some personal work I need to work on to embrace some of these ideas. Do you get naturally excited about these new technologies and just want to jump in and try to learn and understand or are you more hesitant and reserved to some of these things?\n\nBethany Evans (00:09:59): That's a good question, and honestly both. I think I personally am someone who learns by doing. I've seen so many glossaries out there or Twitter threads of people who are very kindly trying to explain all of this lingo. But as you said, there's a lot of lingo. It's a lot of terms. And the only way I learn personally is by doing, I have just been getting in there. One of my friends actually is part of the team, a team working on a particular NFT project, and I won't say which one, but he told me about it and it was a really good chance for me to invest at a very, very low level, pretty risk averse, but I had to set up a crypto account and figure out how to buy crypto and then transfer that to my wallet and then follow this particular NFT project on Twitter, which leads to their Discord community that they've been building out, and then being part of the premin and then the Mint experience. (00:11:00): And I know that there is so much I still don't know, but actually just jumping in and doing it again with very low levels of money involved has been so helpful for me to at least start to get a grasp on this as we work out our North Face strategy and I see what different brands are tackling. I think there's always that element of when anything new comes out. Yeah, it's scary. Sometimes you just want to bury your head in the sand and be like, I cannot learn one more thing. On the flip side, it can be really fun and exciting once you do start to understand it. And yeah, sometimes I think you just have to dive in and do it versus reading about it, if that makes sense.", "token_estimate": 529, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:09:31", "end_timestamp": "00:09:59", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "b624a341-ce2e-4099-8b17-3db71613e318", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:11:44): Sure. Well, and I think in the back of our mind we're all realizing, oh, this could be the next Instagram and therefore maybe I should take this seriously because if I get in on the ground floor, I could really ride this wave. Alternatively, it could become what was that service that Google offered or it was like Google Plus or\n\nBethany Evans (00:12:01): Yes, Google Plus\n\nCole Heilborn (00:12:04): Where things maybe it's more of a flop and you're like, well, do I want to invest a bunch of time into something that might not pan out? So I'm curious at the North Face in the halls in meetings, what are some of the common technologies that are coming up that are being discussed frequently that you guys are watching or dabbling in?", "token_estimate": 191, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:11:44", "end_timestamp": "00:12:04", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "16feaa8a-aac5-4640-b04c-c77077580350", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Bethany Evans (00:12:24): Great question. I guess I do want to acknowledge what you said around the scary elements of it, because it's true, there are already so many frauds and scams coming out. People have been comparing NFTs to Beanie Babies. Are a lot of these projects going to fail? Yes. Are there going to be a lot of learnings and a lot of iterations? Yes. So I think as we talk around the halls, that's part of it. Is this just too scary? Can a big company get into this space? I really see four different buckets for the big brand experience opportunities in the Metaverse, which would be one is customizable avatars and NFTs, this idea of digital and combining the physical and digital worlds, and do you have virtual clothing? I saw a quote and one mom said that her teenage daughter was spending more on virtual clothing for her avatars than on real clothing. (00:13:24): Wow. I mean, that's a shift from when I was a teenager. Second would be virtual and hybrid events as we all went to virtual events over the past two years, and I'm sure our CFOs appreciated the lower travel budgets, but we realized a lot of these conferences, a lot of these industry events, these speaker events, could they be virtual if people don't want to travel or could we at least offer them in a hybrid way where if you're not able to travel, there's also so many issues of accessibility. So I think those are some of the positives that have come out of the pandemic is do we keep the good things and combine them with the old things that we all miss to create something new? So events would be the second one. Third one would be this idea of tokens and brand access tokens and the idea that you might purchase an NFT, which gives you access to a special collaboration or product drop or shopping preview, things like that. (00:14:27): And then the fourth and final one, which I think is more of the OG, is gaming integrations. If we think back to Fortnite and Roblox and all of these games is kind of being on the forefront of this whole idea of the metaverse, which they have been, that is probably the lowest lift in many ways because games already exist. They already have built-in communities, they have built-in ways for brands to work with them. So that is certainly one of the less risky ways to get into this space. But yes, it's interesting, and I recently read something that really resonated from one of our agency partners, and they were doing a consumer survey and just looking at how are consumers feeling coming out of two years of the pandemic and all of these changes that are happening. And there was this idea of future shock that came up, this idea that it can be distressing to have too many rapid accelerations in society and technology happening all at once. And I do think there's that element of that. Exactly as you said, a lot of this can feel scary. It's a lot of new terminology that can feel exclusionary or just very difficult to learn. There's this idea, I think of Crypto Bros and is this something that's accessible to everyone and should it be since the whole idea of Web3 is decentralization? So yes, it's a lot that's being talked about, but I would go back to those four buckets as what seemed to be the initial four ways for brands to think about activating.", "token_estimate": 823, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:12:24", "end_timestamp": "00:12:24", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "a3a0a7e1-8802-4607-ab77-f4e1188c29bb", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:16:11): Interesting. So the buckets are to recap clothing and in metaverse purchases, events, tokens for brand access for physical product, and then gaming interactions, and I can't think of the right word. Is there a word for it?\n\nBethany Evans (00:16:28): Is there a word for that? Integration.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:16:29): Integrations. Okay. Interesting.\n\nBethany Evans (00:16:33): Yeah, and I'd be happy to talk about the one we did with Roblox because we had some good learnings coming out of that.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:16:40): So I do want to get there maybe before we get there, do you see all of these taking place in the Metaverse or are these all in their own worlds?", "token_estimate": 173, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:16:11", "end_timestamp": "00:16:40", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "47981762-d15b-4846-91ce-da78b244dfc1", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Bethany Evans (00:16:51): That's a good question too because I think we tend to equate the metaverse with a lot of other things that are adjacent. And I'm certainly guilty of this too. I hear these different terms, and I think we sometimes are using it interchangeably with Web3 point, but that technically those are two different things, and I've heard them described different ways. One that I really like that is really simple is that Web 1.0 was the read web, right? We started to read our newspapers or blogs or there was very tightly controlled content. And Web 1.0 let us access that for the first time in a virtual way. We didn't have to get a physical newspaper anymore or a physical newsletter from our college, for example. And then that Web 2.0 was the read and write web. Now you could start to write maybe you're engaging blogs or forums or social media, but it was kind of that two-way exchange, but still very much with gatekeepers, the Facebooks now the Metas of the world, the different large conglomerates. (00:18:03): And so I think it's interesting that Web3 0.0 then is the read, write and Own web. And I think that piece around ownership is so interesting because the hope is that Web3 0.0 can solve for the idea of ownership over digital things. And it's funny, my husband is an intellectual property attorney, so of course he's already asking and seeing in his world, but wait, how does IP exist in this world? And if there's a claim of fraud, who do you go to if you say, Hey, my NFT was stolen somehow somebody else got the access credentials and now they say they own it. What do you even do about it in that case? So it's this really interesting idea, and it feels like this cultural shift where gaming has paved the way. Going back to your question about metaverse, we're seeing all of the building blocks to get us to that decentralized vision. So whether it's blockchain, crypto and wallets, NFTs, those are all I think building blocks that you would apply to the Metaverse to create the brand experience that you want. Whether that's roadblocks or some of these more sophisticated executions we're starting to see. But yeah, it's so much newness all at once.", "token_estimate": 548, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:16:51", "end_timestamp": "00:16:51", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "cef6f0bc-46b3-411e-a596-c8dbbf5ae71e", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:19:26): So are we officially in the Web3 phase? Have we crushed that threshold, or is it still in the future?\n\nBethany Evans (00:19:34): It doesn't feel like it to me. I'm sure there are some people who would argue differently. It seems like it's maybe a few more years out before we truly are blending the physical and the digital in a more seamless way. Still, it feels pretty clunky if you've tried some of those virtual avatar meeting rooms. I mean, my kids have an Oculus and it's really fun and it's improved so much over how it used to be, but to me it still feels like we have a little ways to go.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:20:04): Gotcha. So when we're talking about the Metaverse, we're not just talking about how would you define the Metaverse?\n\nBethany Evans (00:20:15): It's good question. I think to me, the Metaverse is just this idea of a virtual existence that you can opt into the degree that you want, which I think will vary so much person to person. You might have someone who loves living in the virtual world. And again, I think it's so interesting when you go back to the ideas of accessibility, and that's one thing we've been thinking about for the North Face. We're a brand that our tagline is never stop exploring. How does that idea of exploration translate to the Metaverse? You think of something like Ready Player One. Okay, that is a very advanced version of this idea of you could explore in different worlds that have all of these different themes based on pop culture or whatever else. So I don't know that I have a good definition to be honest with you, other than I think it's this virtual idea that Neil Stevenson introduced back in the snow crash. But I think how it actually comes to life and all of this idea of real estate in the metaverse and artificial scarcity, and again, degree of participation that'll probably look very individual, which seems like the whole promise. So yeah, I'm excited by it, but I also have a lot of questions about it.", "token_estimate": 501, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:19:26", "end_timestamp": "00:20:15", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "48235874-1be4-464e-b919-3fad47b33003", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:21:37): Okay. Wow. Yeah. So maybe this is a good chance to segue into talking about the Roblox Vans example. Could you give us a little background on that? And maybe just for folks who don't know your affiliation with Vans, because I feel like that seems like a really kind of great case study or example that has provided some insights into some of these things, right?\n\nBethany Evans (00:22:01): Yes. So I am very lucky to be at the North Face. We're owned by the F Corporation, which owns fans, Timberland Supreme, and a number of other brands. And it's my first time working for a portfolio company. And what I've loved about it is in the past, if I wanted to talk to another company, there were NDAs and all of the paperwork. And with being part of a portfolio company, we can just talk to our sister companies and share information and share projects and data without the need for that level of governance. And so yeah, vans introduced a Van's world within Roblox, which made a ton of sense given their demographic target, which is different from ours. Of course, a huge number of people love Vans. I think everyone from little kids to older adults, so don't mean to say that they do not serve a wide variety of the population, but Roblox, the consumer tends to be on the younger side. I think it's like eight to 12 in there.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:23:05): And what is Roblox? What is that?", "token_estimate": 353, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:21:37", "end_timestamp": "00:23:05", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "9a5a1259-8e42-409e-b3d5-d6ba3c59a874", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Bethany Evans (00:23:08): Yeah, so it's a virtual gaming world, kind of like Minecraft for those who might be familiar with the Minecraft instead of a Roblox. But you have an avatar within Roblox, and there are many different experiences where your avatar can go to participate. So what is really cool about it is a lot of brands have built out their own worlds, worlds within the larger Roblox world. And so Vans is one of them. Last year they very scrappily and quickly, a lot of it was they worked with developers over Slack and they were just trading stuff back and forth all day on Slack is what they shared with us. So I would just say that to say I can't share specifics, but from what they told us, the cost of entry, the development time, it was all much cheaper and much faster than I would've expected. So I think again, sometimes we have in our heads ideas that, oh, only big brands can afford to do X, Y, or Z. (00:24:13): And having worked in both startups earlier in my career and then bigger brands later in my career, I don't think that's true. I think sometimes smaller brands can be incredibly successful doing these so-called bigger activations. You just have to be more choiceful. If you do have a lower overall budget and you have 10 opportunities on the table in front of you, maybe a bigger brand can afford to do eight, and a smaller brand can afford to do two or three, but that doesn't mean they can't do an incredible job at the two or three that they choose. I think again, it's just thinking about product market fit. And for Vans, this made a ton of sense because they had the opportunity to build out, they're so known. Of course, Tony Hawk known for being the skateboarding community is kind of how they originated and grew up. (00:25:06): And so they had the opportunity to build a virtual skateboarding world within Roblox, which is so true to their DNA, and so true to that more younger demographic within their consumers, keeping Vans top of mind. So yes, vans told us about this van's world that they launched and has been very successful. And we said, Ooh, that's really cool. And my colleague Zena worked with them, and we launched a popup North Face store within the Van's world. So now you're having a virtual pop-up store within another brand's experience, and it was all winterized. So it was the idea of come and dress your avatar in North Face gear for the winter. And it was just really cool. I haven't seen the final readout yet, but initial results were quite promising. We did not know, again, given our demographic, would any of these kids playing Roblox be interested or would our popup be a total bust? (00:26:07): In which case, okay, fine, we learned something. And so I think we've been surprised by the amount of engagement. And again, this was a very low lift, very easy way to start to dip our toes into that space. Not a lot of development work, not a lot of time or money, but really cool to just test stuff. And I think ultimately that's a way to protect yourself a bit is just testing in different small ways to start. You don't always have to go with this giant fully baked out strategy with all kinds of new technology because what has become very clear to me and my colleagues within marketing as we start to talk about the Metaverse Web3 0.0 is it is not just a marketing conversation within marketing. If we want to throw an event, we can just go through an event. This is so different. (00:27:03): We need to talk to legal, we need to talk to the privacy team, we need to talk to the web development team. Everything in this space touches so many teams that you cannot activate on it in a vacuum. So I think it's very good in a way because it makes you work very cross-functionally internally. The flip side of that, especially at a big company to be candid, is it can be slow, right? And I know you and I talked a little bit about our VF Venture Foundry and kind of the whole idea behind that to try to create some agility even within the framework of a larger company. That's been really encouraging. And they've brought us some partners and some technologies to consider that they've already pre-vetted, pre considered those. So yes, we're working on it.", "token_estimate": 1057, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:23:08", "end_timestamp": "00:23:08", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "1e29eeda-717d-4d1c-a07c-dc81bd8c8b25", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:27:53): So with your pop-up store, I could take my avatar to your store and am I purchasing North Face apparel or am I just with in-game credits, or am I just going and browsing through things and throwing them on my avatar? And are these actual products that you guys make or are they designed specifically for the game?\n\nBethany Evans (00:28:14): These have been 3D versions of actual products that we make. Of course, you can absolutely design online exclusives for virtual worlds, which is something I've already seen a lot of brands start to do, which is really cool. There's even brands that are saying, buy this physical product like this jacket and will also give you the virtual version of it for free is kind of an add-on, which is so interesting. But yes, there is actual, at least with Roblox, there is real money involved. They are purchasing these items using in game credits, which they're spending real money to buy just like you do with so many of these games. And then again, I can't get into the revenue specifics, but for most of these experiences, there's some sort of revenue sharing opportunity that's set up where the brand is actually getting dollars back and they can get those development costs back potentially within a matter of months, and then turn these experiences into a revenue stream, which I think anytime you can speak positively to the bottom line, that's the win. (00:29:28): But for me, especially, it's been so interesting the last couple years with all of the supply chain challenges. I think every retailer has dealt with those products coming in later than you had planned on, or maybe not as much as you had planned on. And how do you deal with that? How do you get stuff to the consumer's hands? How do you adjust all of your forecast? And the idea that you can have virtual goods that aren't dependent on the real world supply chain and are a little bit safer in terms of a projectable revenue stream. That is very interesting too.", "token_estimate": 500, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:27:53", "end_timestamp": "00:28:14", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "b70527ab-b508-4714-837c-58e11e9cb0f9", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:30:03): Well, that seems like it opens up, well, I guess obviously a whole new world to something where you're outside of startup and then maintenance costs, if you could turn a profit on all of your digital goods, that seems like, well, I don't know, that's kind of blowing my mind right now to think that a company like the North Face could add that as a revenue stream in a substantial component of that as well.\n\nBethany Evans (00:30:28): And again, it's early days. How big could this potentially be? I don't know. But it does feel like you said, it's very intriguing.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:30:37): So with the Van's example, I guess, I don't know, what did you learn from that and what does that make you wonder about or how are you taking this experience and applying some of these lessons to future ideas or projects?", "token_estimate": 210, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:30:03", "end_timestamp": "00:30:37", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "dfbde9c8-b390-45fb-a8d7-79b3eb48cdfa", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Bethany Evans (00:30:56): Definitely a big thing we're thinking about is the utility and what is the value exchange? If we're asking consumers to do something like, yeah, technically use their money or their parents' money to buy goods, do those feel like they're actually worth it? And then when you start to think about some of these other things like, Hey, give us your data and we'll give you this NFT or this token or whatever it is. I think with all of these privacy changes that are happening, which need to happen, the idea that in the future people will have crypto wallets which own their digital identity and that they would have more control over how that digital identity gets distributed. I think you have to think about with anything you're planning, and there's some things we're planning I can't talk about yet, but we are really, really thinking about A, what is the infrastructure required to actually build this ourselves versus buy it? There's always that build versus buy consideration. And Nike famously recently bought what was considered the, I heard it described as the Nike of the Metaverse, a company that was pretty advanced in that space, and then Nike bought 'em because Nike can afford to and kudos to them for moving quickly. (00:32:21): But yeah, it's the build versus buy, and then it's that value exchange of one of the things that I manage being our loyalty program. Of course, I see so many applications with all of this within the loyalty space, are we able to better consumer's preferences and cater to those preferences in a whole new way? But then what are we asking them to give up? Are we asking them for more data or different data? And what are we providing in return that makes that feel like a fair value exchange to them? I think we've all been burned, right? Sometimes you join a brand and you feel like you're giving them all this information. Maybe they ask for your birthday, but then they never send you anything on your birthday. So you're like, well, why did you want to know other than for your own database, but what are you giving me back as the consumer if we're giving you that information? (00:33:13): So that's a lot of what I'm thinking about along with the globalization idea. It was so funny. We had a call and we were talking about all of the things we were working on for North America in this space, and then our media team was like, great. We're also talking about a lot of these things over in Europe. And our APAC team said the same thing, same here over in Asia. And we all just had a collective moment of, whoa. We have worked pretty regionally in the past in many ways because thinking about your regional supply chain, your regional distribution, the whole idea that everything about the metaverse is global and could be accessed for the most part by anyone, anywhere, that's kind of mind blowing. But then going back to those regulatory issues, well, what regulations apply if you're trying to create an experience that is global, that it's being consumed by people in different countries with different privacy laws, different regulations, our poor legal team, I think every legal team is grappling with this, trying to understand. In many cases, there is not established law, there are not established guidelines. Everyone's trying to figure this out. And the brands that move really quickly, some of them will get burned, but on the flip side, they have an opportunity to be an early mover and potentially build out a really great community quickly. So it's a lot to think about", "token_estimate": 890, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:30:56", "end_timestamp": "00:30:56", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "f8edfb6f-e83d-4bc7-a1ce-33b9de37a3cf", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:34:48): When I think about the cultural nuances too. I feel like I don't even know how you'd begin to try to consider that part of the equation as well.\n\nBethany Evans (00:34:56): True,\n\nCole Heilborn (00:34:56): But huh.\n\nBethany Evans (00:34:57): So many nuances.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:34:59): Yeah. So you talked a little bit about NFTs. What are other use cases that you see NFTs being applicable?\n\nBethany Evans (00:35:08): I think another really interesting one is this idea of co-creation and micro influencers, and again, building versus I guess in this case, borrowing. I have really enjoyed watching. I think for example, Adidas, they've partnered with communities like Board ape, yacht Club, and Crypto Punks, kind of those, I think what they're calling next generation influencers where these communities have already been built. There are some of the early big communities in this space with very active members who it seems like in many cases are willing to embrace or at least give brands a chance when they're trying to activate with these members. As long as it's in a way, going back to what we said about providing value. But that is so interesting to me because I've worked in the influencer space for a long time. When I worked at Southwest, we launched what we called our southwest storyteller program because we felt like the influencer space at that time was so fragmented where you were paying an influencer at a time to do a post and there was no ongoing relationship. (00:36:18): And in some cases, they didn't even know much about your brand. It felt inauthentic, and consumers are not stupid. They pick up on that. And so what we decided to do at Southwest was work with those more micro and medium influencers and launched this program called Southwest Storytellers, where when you were picked to be a southwest storyteller, and we tried to pick a very diverse array of people from different backgrounds and different stories to tell, we would give them tickets for a whole year. So they would spend a whole year traveling around on us, deciding what they wanted to talk about, and then they would roll off into our, what we were calling our storyteller alumni network, where it's like, Hey, we don't want to lose that relationship with you, but we do want to make sure we're always giving new people a chance to participate in this program. (00:37:08): But because it was a year long, we actually built these relationships. We hosted all of these storytellers out at our headquarters, gave them tours, answered their questions, and I think it came across in such a better way because it actually was authentic. So same thing right now that we're talking about these opportunities to maybe co-create with creators in this space who they've already built up a following, they know this space better than we do as brands. Is there a way that we can make something together with them or provide something of value to their community, assuming that their community is maybe a target demographic that we're trying to reach? And again, I think it depends on your brand, but those are some other really interesting executions too. I saw another one that I liked from Levi's. They were letting you win N NFTs, so doing sweepstakes to win an NFT to get your jeans repaired for free. So if you had one of these NFTs, you could bring your old ripped jeans in, and I don't know the exact process, but there was real world value that came attached to this virtual item. So again, those blurred lines, it seems to go both directions.", "token_estimate": 891, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:34:48", "end_timestamp": "00:35:08", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "8d5ad390-8978-4709-8993-cbd879d6d2c9", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:38:27): So in that instance, what is their motivation? Are they just trying to get people into the NFT world by fixing your Holy genes? Is that the goal there?\n\nBethany Evans (00:38:39): I believe so in offering different ways for their community to engage with them. And that's what it all goes back to. I think all of this is community. It's been so interesting for me, that example I shared with you of my friends NFT project, and I've gotten involved in the Discord community and I've joined a few others. They are so active. And as someone who has worked in the social space for a long time, that is unusual, right? A lot of communities are dormant or toxic or veer off from their original purpose in a way that might or might not make sense. But the fact that there is so much energy and excitement in this space right now, so many people, sure, there's plenty of people who are just flipping NFTs to make money, and if that's what they want to do, great more power to them. I think there's a second group of people who seem to be investing in these areas because they're really excited about the possibilities and the community and the idea of connecting with like-minded individuals who love this idea of decentralization and giving more power to the people. And so I think there's a lot there. But yes, I would say it all goes back to community. Is this a new way to engage with members or at least a subset of your community?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:39:58): So why does an NFT help foster a community? Why are those two connected?", "token_estimate": 389, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:38:27", "end_timestamp": "00:39:58", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "1e6754d4-d5f0-49e7-8710-34368493bfca", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Bethany Evans (00:40:05): I think part of it's like anything, right? Well, there's artificial scarcity. Usually when they're minting these collections, they're saying, oh, we have 5,000 for 10,000. So right there, you're part of a club. You're one of the only 5,000 holders of whatever this particular NFT might be. And a lot of times within the communities, they have even subsets within that. Okay, within this 5,000 NFT, how rare is yours? And all of us that have this type of rare creation, we're in this little group, but it's all tribes. I think as marketers, we're always just trying to understand what do our consumers care about and how do we give them what they care about in a way that makes sense for our brand? And so much of that has always been around this idea that your consumer isn't one person. It's different tribes of people and very individual people. (00:40:58): And I love those examples where they'll say, Hey, here's two people. Who are they? Both Bethany and Stephanie. They both live in Colorado. They're both 35. They both on paper. From a basic demographic perspective, you might look exactly the same, but then you try to dig deeper into those psychographics and those motivations and you realize, oh, they're actually completely different and you should speak to them in completely different ways. And that is really interesting to me and something that I think this whole new Web3 world could potentially enable or facilitate in a bit of a different way\n\nCole Heilborn (00:41:38): Generating these more micro communities, these more climates.", "token_estimate": 394, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:40:05", "end_timestamp": "00:41:38", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "be698a17-ea15-4274-a75b-3556c2e67ead", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Bethany Evans (00:41:42): Yeah, but I think, again, with so much power in the communities as it should be, brands have to be authentic social. You still had a little bit of control in terms of you could kind of, well, you could control, I'll just go back to the old days of Facebook pages, for example. You controlled who was allowed to your page. You could remove followers, you could remove posts that went against your community guidelines that were on your Facebook page. There were sort of some built-in controls for brands. If you're a brand trying to be part of an existing discord community, it's kind of like with Reddit, anytime a brand does an A MA on Reddit, wow, that could go really well or really poorly. It's great, but people say, ask me anything seriously. It's like, are you really prepared to answer anything that they're going to ask? I think there's a little bit, I think a lot of Reddit folks are probably also Web3 folks. I don't know what that Venn diagram looks like, but yeah, just keep it real.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:42:51): So yeah, you bring up a good point. If a brand is letting go of some of its control in these communities, how does it make sure that it shows up authentically and that it is a showing up in its true self?\n\nBethany Evans (00:43:07): I think one would be putting culture before technology. We talked a little bit about, there's so much tech behind all of this, but the technology is just an enabler for any of these examples. Who is using the technology? Why and let that drive. I think the decisions versus, oh, here's a new technology capability. Let's use it. Without that decision being driven by, no, no, no, what's the cultural component? Like why and how people are engaging with this. And if you don't know ask. I have gotten much more comfortable over the years at being okay with what I don't know. Again, brands don't know a lot about this, and a lot of people don't know a lot about this. Find the experts, ask them. They tend to be so kind and willing about sharing their knowledge and inviting people into their spaces. I think it's okay to know what you don't know and not be the all-knowing all powerful marketers or brand not.", "token_estimate": 548, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:41:42", "end_timestamp": "00:43:07", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "f31c6ede-27f0-4286-9d0c-1cf7a85ed8f6", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:44:15): Yeah. Gosh, yeah. This conversation's making my head hurt a little bit about all this stuff.\n\nBethany Evans (00:44:21): I hear you.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:44:23): I was reading this article earlier today, and it was some research firm had predicted that extended reality will exceed, it'll be an 18 billion industry by 2023, which I don't know. I'm curious to hear your thoughts on that.\n\nBethany Evans (00:44:40): I can certainly believe it. I know that it's been a big area in the VC space as well, but in that way, it feels a little bit like the.com era. I think everyone's recognizing there's a bubble. Will some of it burst? Will some people lose money? I think inevitably, yes. So you have to wonder how that's going to play out. Do people get a little bit wary for a while? It is this new bright, shiny object, and inevitably that's going to come down to earth and people are going to be like, all right. It's like anything else. There's pros and cons, but yes, the growth seems to be exponential. Exactly as you said, everything that I'm reading and seeing is exactly the same, that we're kind of at the point of adoption and acceleration of adoption where it's only going to keep going up at least for a while\n\nCole Heilborn (00:45:35): With all of this. And we talked, you mentioned it earlier, the problem of privacy. It also sounds like though could privacy in 2022, it could be considered a new technology, how we're thinking about privacy, how we're responding to a push for privacy in the digital space.", "token_estimate": 385, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:44:15", "end_timestamp": "00:45:35", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "b998a128-ae3f-4154-8a70-628f3cd76b4c", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Bethany Evans (00:45:57): Definitely. I think there's the money exchange and the data exchange and this question of how do we tie in digital and physical realities and how do we manage data through walled gardens? We've always had this issue as marketers in the 2.0 world of social where you have these walled gardens like Google and Facebook and they don't talk to each other. And how are you finding your customers across these different walled gardens? I think the exact same thing will happen in Web3 0.0, and it will depend on the level of access that people choose to give us now instead of companies in the future. It won't be no, Facebook is saying you can't have that data. The north face, it'll be no coal is saying you can't have that data the north face. So that's a really, really scary but also exciting idea. I always have to sometimes take off my marketing hat and just think of how do I feel as a consumer and what would I be comfortable being shared or known about me? So it's a really interesting idea, even this idea of friendship and less differentiation between digital friends and real world friends. And as a parent, how does that play in? I personally am very hesitant to let my youngest join games like Fortnite because I don't know who he's talking to or what they're talking about or who these people actually are. And I think all of those questions, they're not going away. There's even this idea of taxes. (00:47:35): How are taxes going to work and what about the secondary market? And so yeah, I think there are a lot of questions about all of this.", "token_estimate": 396, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:45:57", "end_timestamp": "00:45:57", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "f238cd5f-47ae-48e5-b779-c3352c49ff16", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:47:44): So all this sounds cool, all this new technology sounds fun, and it sounds like it'll be a great time once it becomes a reality. I guess what I'm curious now about though is for a brand like the North Face, as you said, the tagline has never stopped exploring. And so whether that's into the mountains or into the digital realm, I guess if you're exploring, then you are fulfilling the brand. Is there a point though, where the digital just becomes, you start to lose the core of what the north face is, and you have to say, no, we're actually not going to go any further. We're going to continue to push into the real world. How do you balance those?", "token_estimate": 170, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:47:44", "end_timestamp": "00:47:44", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "8557746d-bc5e-44d2-92a8-c5200f968a8d", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 22, "content": "Bethany Evans (00:48:27): That's the line we're walking. Absolutely. I think there's been really interesting examples like Pokemon Go and the idea of ar where sometimes you can overlay that virtual component on the real world experiences, but you're still encouraging people to get out there and go places and see things. Those are really interesting. Another thing that we're grappling with is the environmental aspect of all of this. NFTs use a lot of energy when they're what's called minted a, a tokenizing a digital file within blockchain, or at least the original blockchain technology. So we're thinking about, okay, sure, there's different offset utilities that exist to make an activation net zero or even negative, like you plant a tree for each NFT purchase, right? There's offset, things like that. Or you can use a lower consumption blockchain. I think polygon would be one or proof of stake, but that's another thing we're considering is we're a brand that very much cares about keeping the outside world beautiful and sustainable. (00:49:39): And we recognize that retail in general has not been kind to the environment. We have certainly been culpable in that, and we've made a lot of changes and are continuing to make a lot of changes to try to make everything we do much more renewable, participate much more in the circular economy where we have different trade-in programs and repair programs and our lifetime warranty program. But yeah, that for us is another huge consideration is like you said, if we're about exploration, what does that exploration look like for the virtual world? And then if that exploration is built on the back of these high energy consumption technologies that are not good for the environment, is there a way we can participate in a better way that doesn't go against our core beliefs? And so yeah, I think those considerations are going to be different for every brand based on what does their brand stand for or care about, but a hundred percent, it's a huge thing to think about. So yeah, I would say we have not rushed in, we are working on all of this, and I've given you a couple little examples, but we have not been one of the earliest movers because there are big questions that we're concerned about and we're trying to take our time and do things the right way. Can't promise, we'll never make any missteps, but I think we don't want to rush in and do things that feel completely antithetical to who we are.", "token_estimate": 617, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:48:27", "end_timestamp": "00:48:27", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "053500d6-5eac-4b02-8cd9-34f480b4b4a2", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 23, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:51:13): Do you have a way of evaluating what technologies are worth investing in? I don't know if you have a corporate rubric or if you personally have a rubric that you use to try to evaluate some of these things. I dunno, maybe something that people can use to kind of implement in their own scenario if they're trying to validate or understand some of these things.\n\nBethany Evans (00:51:35): That's such a good one. Yes, we do have different evaluation criteria. I'll say the first thing that we do is we don't act in a vacuum anytime. We're evaluating new technology. We work with our central VF technology partners, and again, this is more specific to portfolio companies, but we try to think about the data flow from the outset, and that is the most unsexy thing ever. But almost all of these technologies, they have some sort of data component where files are being shared, maybe consumer data is being shared, maybe there's APIs. And we are increasingly so protective of consumer data and some of these different metaverse activations that are underway would consider some sort of data exchange. And are these new companies, they're not the legacy companies that already have a lot of this security documentation and privacy documentation in place. So the biggest thing we do to start with is literally just map out the data flows of very simply, what is the use case for this technology? (00:52:48): And that's another thing is trying to separate the use case from the specific provider. Because a lot of times there's multiple providers that could do the same thing and you don't want to get so locked in on one that you're losing side of the forest for the trees. So we try to evaluate to start, not necessarily do we have this one partner in mind that we want to work with, but what are we trying to achieve for our consumer? What does success look like? And then what would need to be true for that success to occur? And a lot of times it is those literal flows and sometimes in those flows discover blockers where it's like, Hey, in order for this to work, we would need a real-time API, but we couldn't do an API or the partner couldn't do an API for X, Y, Z reason. (00:53:35): So it would be really slow and it would be terrible consumer experience. It's those tactical questions that sometimes uncover really, really important things. So yeah, I guess that's my advice is have the big idea. That's awesome, but then get really technical in terms of how that big idea would come to life and be prepared to pivot. I think as marketers, we pivot all the time. You have something in your head and you find out, yes, but or yes. And sometimes it ends up being even better than you initially envisioned, but I think you have to be willing to be flexible, especially in these new worlds where the regulations, the technology, it's all changing by the day.", "token_estimate": 722, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:51:13", "end_timestamp": "00:51:35", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "453ae9a0-fc58-41ee-9c63-d0eb2d3b00d4", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 24, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:54:23): Are there any brands out there in the outdoor space who you're kind of watching and are really curious to see how some of these projects are going? Is there anyone dabbling in this that you're paying attention to? I know you've mentioned some brands outside the outdoors, but anyone inside the industry,\n\nBethany Evans (00:54:43): I don't know that I've seen anyone within the industry. Of course, we all pay attention to each other. I haven't seen anyone do a ton in this space yet. And I would imagine it's because a lot of them are asking the same questions that are kind of unique to our industry. So that's great. I think we're all probably taking a similar approach to, cool, let's get into the space, but let's do it carefully. Outside of the outdoor industry, they've been really impressed with Gucci. We've partnered with them on different product collaborations, but I've just always been so impressed with how they're consistently on the forefront of these new technologies pushing the envelope. They also did a ROBLOX execution, but they're one I would just encourage anyone to pay attention to. And also, I think we all have to be honest as marketers and share with each other what are the issues we encounter that we can learn from because there are failures that could easily happen to anyone. (00:55:45): And I think an example of that would be McDonald's. I have a dear friend who recently worked at McDonald's, so this is in no way a knock on them. I think they're an amazing brand, but they famously had an NFT project for their McRibs where some bad actor had embedded racial slurs deep within the blockchain data, and it came out later. And how do you plan for something like that and start to understand even that this is an area of watch out, like, oh, you need to comb through every bit of this blockchain data. And the idea that blockchain is a permanent record, that doesn't go away. I think as marketers, we've gotten used to the ability to update things. Maybe there's an error on our website and we're able to update it and just send consumers a notification that like, Hey, this was wrong. (00:56:38): We fixed it. Here's the new correct information. You can't do that with blockchain. You can send out the apology email, but whatever's on the blockchain is still on the blockchain, at least as far as my understanding. So again, that's not something I would call a failure of McDonald's. That was them experimenting and finding out something that now we're all like, oh, okay, that's something I should watch out for too. And I know we all, as marketers, we have so many different forums. You're providing an awesome forum. We have adage, Adweek, whatever it might be. But I think the more transparent we are with each other about, Hey, here's the potential pitfall, or here's something that I uncovered, it's going to help all of us and help everyone at the C-suite get a little bit more comfortable too, knowing that exchange of information and exchange of best practices is happening just happened in the social space. I mean, I remember when I started in social, there were no best practices, there were no playbooks, there were no industry organizations. And then slowly organizations started coming out, like social media.org that provided a place for marketers to talk to each other and be like, Hey, are you dealing with this too? Or did you see that? And we're going to need the exact same thing for this space.", "token_estimate": 870, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:54:23", "end_timestamp": "00:54:43", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "f9a58062-50e8-4bbe-89e5-cb08dfa20c98", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 25, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:57:55): Yeah. What gets you the most excited, thinking about the future and the technology that lies ahead of us, what sort of opportunities do you see or would you love to see? But there's no roadmap as to how to get there currently?\n\nBethany Evans (00:58:11): Well, I would say of course I'm excited because a huge sci-fi and fantasy nerd, I grew up watching Star Trek, the Next Generation with my dad. That was our bonding thing. Every week we would watch TNG, and I've always read a ton of science, fantasy, and fiction. So again, going back to Danielle Stevensons of the world and the William Gibson's. So it's kind of amazing that a lot of these ideas I've been reading about for the last 20, 30 years are potentially at least closer than they used to be. And I really, really do love the idea that it could kind of democratize some areas in a new way. I think that is a very powerful thing that could potentially be really good. So I'm very excited about the applications and where all of this could go, but I'm very wary, again, having lived through social, lived through the.com bust. I'm kind of dating myself now, but I'm in my late thirties. I've seen a couple booms and busts, and I think right now it's the new shiny object like we've talked about. Inevitably there will be issues that come out, issues of fraud, money that gets lost, scammers, and how do you address those issues that continue to move forward? And just a really positive, productive way, I think is something we just all need to try to drive as an industry, because then I think, again, there could be so much good that comes out of it.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:59:44): Yeah, and who knows, maybe in two or three years if the podcast is still going, conversations, this will be happening in a 3D virtual world rather than sitting on Zoom.\n\nBethany Evans (00:59:56): Exactly. How crazy would that be?\n\nCole Heilborn (00:59:59): That would be pretty cool. The think about that is pretty wild because I feel like when I think about the use cases of the metaverse, most of my reaction is pretty negative, but that particular interaction seems really cool.", "token_estimate": 537, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "00:57:55", "end_timestamp": "00:59:59", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "7239ac40-3acc-463f-ac97-7d30d4cc92d9", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 26, "content": "Bethany Evans (01:00:14): Yeah, I think so too. I've even heard people talking about social clubs within the Metaverse, and that feels like the idea of taking stuff like the Discord group you're in or the subreddit you follow, and just moving some of that dialogue to where there's still a really low barrier to entry. You're not having to get in your car and drive somewhere 30 or 45 minutes if you don't want to, but here's another way that you can be interactive and be social with people. And I will be honest, that's something that very much worries me as a mom. My 12-year-old is not allowed on social media yet. He's one of the very few among his friend group. But again, I've seen the dark side of it and I'm like, no, no, you can wait a little bit longer. But there has been some stuff lost, I think, with the younger generation and just not interacting in person as much as we used to when we were kids. And is this maybe an option that kind of bridges some of that a little bit more? Because I know we can't go backwards. There's no point in bemoaning the past and in so many ways, the younger generation is amazing. I hear my son and his friends talking about the environment, and they're just so much more aware than I ever was at that age. But yeah, how do we offer them new technologies and new ways of engaging?", "token_estimate": 332, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "01:00:14", "end_timestamp": "01:00:14", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "b90e1c6f-5b54-46fd-b9a4-308fc9162a7c", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 27, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:01:36): I think about something like 360 video, which I feel like was really hot five or seven years ago, and I don't know, I still see it pop up here and there, but it seems like it's kind of hit its peak. I feel like GoPro came out with their 360 camera and then when that didn't really convert everyone, it just kind of seemed to fall flat. But I do wonder, well, I guess I have a couple thoughts. One, I wonder if the Metaverse will revitalize 360 video and make it actually secure its place in as a tool. But I also wonder, I don't know if many of these tools will just kind of become another gimmick. And I don't want to say that 360 8 is a gimmick, but it seems like it's like, oh, that'd be cool, but is that really, do we need that? Is that necessary? I don't know. But I suppose for every, I don't know what the ratio is, a thousand gimmick technologies. There's one or two that end up sticking around and changing everything.\n\nBethany Evans (01:02:36): Yes, and I think you're right. That is such a good point. I had forgotten all about that, but yeah, five or six years ago, that was 100% the new thing, 360 video. Google had done that really cool 360 video for National Parks, and I think we were all looking at that, being like, whoa, how do I do that for my brand? And then we found out, oh, it's really expensive, and do consumers even want to engage with this versus just going to that place themselves, like you said. Yeah, I'm sure there will be an element of consolidation and some stuff will fall off, but again, I don't want to lead with technology. I think there needs to be a reason to use something versus just the, oh, the technology's there, let's use it. Which was kind of how we all were with 360 video for a little bit. So yeah, it's such a good point.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:03:26): And maybe I feel like the best example of 360 video working is Google Street View, but now that you mentioned it, there is a clear purpose for Street View, and I don't know that there's any other use case where it's as practical other than in street view, which is why it probably still lives and we still like to try to find our houses on Street View.", "token_estimate": 545, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Bethany Evans"], "start_timestamp": "01:01:36", "end_timestamp": "01:03:26", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "0d868620-931e-4ec2-b2f9-7f5ee414d8fd", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 28, "content": "Bethany Evans (01:03:48): I love Street View, but yes, exactly. That is attached to such a real clear utilities. How do we find those moments within this new emerging space? A hundred percent.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:04:00): Well, I'm definitely going to be looking to you and the North Face and your team as we're trying to answer those questions. I feel like you guys are, I don't know, you seem to be, I don't know, leading the charge or you just seem to be really interested in all this stuff, and so I'm curious to see what you guys find and start to implement over the next couple of years as you start to evaluate the landscape and figure out what's worth pursuing.\n\nBethany Evans (01:04:25): Thank you. I'll definitely report back and would love your feedback along the way, because it will very much be iterative. But I think the exciting thing is we have a lot of enthusiasts, and for us, the why matters just as much as the what. So why are we doing this? What are we going to do? But there's a lot of enthusiasm right now, so great. Let's harness it. Let's act on it.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:04:47): Yeah. Are there any resources that you learn from that you follow as you're kind of learning about some of these technologies? Anything that you could point people to if they want to learn more?", "token_estimate": 324, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:03:48", "end_timestamp": "01:04:47", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "90170ea4-cb88-4cf6-871b-4163c9dd62b1", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 29, "content": "Bethany Evans (01:05:00): Honestly, Twitter, it seems like a lot of people in this space are super active on Twitter, and that's been my number one go-to as a marketer my entire career. I'm subscribed to all the industry newsletters. I follow different publications, but I never find as valuable info anywhere else as I do on Twitter where you'll have the practitioner of a subject, whether that's Elon with Tesla or whatever the area is, the actual people doing it will tweet out this amazing information. There's a few different tweet threads from people that are immersed in this space that if you just search in Twitter like NFT or Metaverse, they'll pop up. But that to me, just hearing directly from the source, which is probably, again, part of what I like about Web3 0.0 is just, yeah, it's not filtered through an author or through a publication. It's just real people talking about what they're doing, what's working and what's not. And you can engage with them and ask them questions. And I don't think you should ever be afraid to DM someone with a valid question. I never mind getting those myself. Yeah, it's like, again, let's share information with each other. So Twitter, Twitter's always my recommendation.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:06:13): That's so interesting. I feel like Twitter's kind of on the way out, but here it is, providing resources for what's new and what's up and coming.\n\nBethany Evans (01:06:21): I'll leave very specific use cases, and if you look at what happened with Clubhouse and Twitter spaces, how that's already kind of migrated over. Yeah, I think there's still a very engaged people on Twitter, at least the old guard like me.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:06:37): Awesome. Well, Bethany, I want to thank you for your time and thank you for your willingness just to kind of jump into the deep end on some of this stuff. If folks want to follow along, they want to connect with you or with any of the projects that the North Face has going on, where can people go to connect?", "token_estimate": 501, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:05:00", "end_timestamp": "01:06:37", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "acff1da8-db30-41aa-b0b1-d6c3a9603d2d", "episode_id": "5b06800d-3c82-4449-8b00-380838f8e2df", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c488e", "chunk_index": 30, "content": "Bethany Evans (01:06:53): Yeah, LinkedIn or Twitter? I am at Bethany c Evans on both, and yeah, I would love to hear, thank you, Cole, for having me and listening to me ramble about a space. I'm just learning myself, but you had such good questions and I just appreciate the dialogue.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:07:09): Yeah. Well thank you. I look forward to checking in some amount of time and seeing how some of these projects are going. I think we probably need to do a episode two or a phase two visit in the near future,\n\nBethany Evans (01:07:24): Chapter two. I love it.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:07:26): Alright, well have a great rest of your day.\n\nBethany Evans (01:07:27): You too. Thanks Paul.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:07:29): See you. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 235, "speakers": ["Bethany Evans", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:06:53", "end_timestamp": "01:07:29", "podcast_index": 102, "episode_name": "Ep. 102: How Emerging Technologies Impact Marketers | Bethany Evans | The North Face", "slug": "ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-102-how-emerging-technologies-impact-marketers-bethany-evans-the-north-face", "guest_name": "Bethany Evans", "guest_title": "Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management", "categories": ["industry-trends"], "short_description": "Bethany Evans, Senior Marketing Director of Global Brand Management at The North Face, joins us on the podcast to chat about emerging technologies.", "podcast_length": "1:07:00"}, {"id": "bf84cc05-bbf9-4d01-b198-694ac33163c0", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Erica Aarons (00:02): Through conversational commerce. How can I build relationships with these humans who are buying from our brand, these humans who are enthusiasts of our brand? So what this looks like is texting our customers. I send in our SMS text messaging, there's a text that people get that says like, Hey, I'm Erica, I'm the person you're texting. And when you reply, you get a reply from me so you're not talking to a bot. There's a real human here.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is cool and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Port Side Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn, and today I'm sitting down with Erica Aarons. She is the Retention and Engagement Manager over at Rumpel. Erica, how are you?\n\nErica Aarons (01:16): I'm doing well today. Thanks for having me, Cole.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:19): Yeah, thanks for joining us. Where in the world are you calling in from?\n\nErica Aarons (01:24): I'm based in the Tetons, about a half an hour outside of Jackson Hole, Wyoming, and a little town called Victor, Idaho.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:31): Okay, right on. It sounds like the snow is still pretty heavy there. Are you still getting out for some turns or are you looking for some dirt to get out on these\n\nErica Aarons (01:42): Days? Yeah, I've hit the point where I am looking for some brown pow in my life. We're stuck in the pavement warrior season. There's still skiing to be had and snow in the mountains, and we'll see. The winter just keeps going on and on. It came late.", "token_estimate": 505, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:42", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "e12c56f4-f92f-4575-b19c-da219858478b", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (02:02): Yeah, no kidding. Well, it seems to be sticking around. I was looking at our snowpack report for Mount Baker here in the northwest, and I think the report said we have 20% more snow than we normally would at this time of the year, which I thought was really odd. I feel like it's really, it's just been a very hot spring, and so I was surprised that we have more snow than we typically do.\n\nErica Aarons (02:25): Yeah, we had one probably the lowest season that we've ever had since I've lived here over the last seven or eight years, but the snow, there was some late April snowstorms and people are still getting after it, so we'll see.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:42): Awesome. Well, I'm super excited to have you on the show. We've got a fun topic we're going to dive into, and actually you just, we've chatted before this call today, but you just introduced me to this new phrase, this idea of conversational commerce. I've never heard this before, but we're going to dive straight into this idea of conversational commerce. But before we do so, I'd love if you could kind of give us a quick breakdown of your role with Rumpel and then a little background on the company itself.", "token_estimate": 300, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Erica Aarons"], "start_timestamp": "02:02", "end_timestamp": "02:42", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "253e3d30-6271-4b8a-94c5-c041acd81c85", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Erica Aarons (03:14): So I am the retention and engagement manager at rumpel, and a lot of people probably, if you're not in the e-commerce world, you may have not heard of retention, but retention is basically the contrast to acquisition. And so you have the paid advertising and affiliate and all those kind of people really working to acquire customers. And a lot of my job is communicating to those customers once we have 'em. And so my everyday looks like writing emails and SMS website, popups loyalty program. I run a Facebook group. And so really a lot of these own channels that we are able to talk to our customers, tell them about new products, just tell them, hi, how's it going? That kind of thing. And so I really love, I'll get into it, but I love talking to our customers and I'm always just texting our customers and all that crazy stuff. And so that's kind of what a week in my life looks like. We are a blanket company and we make blankets out of the materials that you may commonly find in sleeping bags or puffy jackets, and we take that and turn them into high performance blankets.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:33): Awesome. Gosh, it must've been like a year ago, I was reading this interview with Rumple's founder, and I can't remember the name. You could probably help me out with that.\n\nErica Aarons (04:43): Yeah, Wiley.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:44): Wiley. Okay. And he was talking about the whole idea behind Rumpel and the whole vision for the brand in itself. And he said something in the interview that I was reading that really stuck out to me, and he said, we're trying to build a brand for an industry that typically doesn't have any sort of brand in the space at all, like blankets and comforters. And those types of products typically aren't products that have associated brands or strong brands, but that's something that sounds like Rumpel is trying to do differently.", "token_estimate": 473, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:14", "end_timestamp": "04:44", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "c18d9696-0d40-4bf1-865a-16258e4f3dcb", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Erica Aarons (05:17): And I think also the blanket is kind of a boring category. It hasn't really been modernized in recent history, you think. And there's of course every now and then a comforter company or nice linen or whatever it may be. But really when it comes down to it, the blankets have been pretty much the same and a lot of the blankets that you have in your house or you're like, I don't know where I got from. I got it from Target or I got it from my grandma's house. But repel iss really on a mission to become the blanket company and a lot of cool, yeah, there's just so many cool things going on and so many blankets that we have beyond the standard original puffy that you might see most commonly.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:08): Right, right. Awesome. And how long have you been with the company?\n\nErica Aarons (06:11): I'm new. I started in November of 2021, and so I've been there for almost six months now, which time flies, but I still feel like I'm learning so much.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:25): Well, and when you were describing your job, I'm curious, so you're in charge of retention and engagement. Does that include social and social posting? I know you talked about you're the person that's talking behind the scenes with customers that include Instagram and sites like that.\n\nErica Aarons (06:41): So we have a different team for that. And so we have a brand coordinator who does social, and our CX team helps a lot with a lot of those tickets and social responses. And so I'm not involved in that on a day-to-day basis, but I manage our Facebook group. And so I'm talking to customers there in that sense, but I'm not the one doing the posting.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:06): I see. Where does that line get drawn or how does it get drawn because they're two different forms of talking to a customer. Right,", "token_estimate": 455, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:17", "end_timestamp": "07:06", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "ba98bf73-e8cd-499f-a474-a8c773923d73", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Erica Aarons (07:12): Definitely. I think really it goes into, I have a background in social media as well, and I think social media in itself is, it's really its own job and there's so many different facets to it, and it's really busy. And so I kind of see where my role comes in is saying, okay, well if I'm looking at retention for the whole company, how am I working with these people? How am I giving my teammates ideas for how we can build on what I'm building and how we can use social as a channel to execute that or our CX as a channel to execute these principles?\n\nCole Heilborn (07:57): Gotcha. Okay. Well, I'd love if you could enlighten us, what is conversational commerce and when did this term come about?", "token_estimate": 180, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:12", "end_timestamp": "07:57", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "dc582999-3158-419d-a11d-684056604c11", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Erica Aarons (08:05): Yeah, conversational commerce is a term that's really new. It's really new in the e-commerce space. And I would argue that it's only six months to a year old. It really kind of started to get coined when SMS marketing started popping into the picture. Text message marketing is a really new channel. You may have signed up for people's texts in the past, but when it comes down to it, our job as marketers is to personify our brands. No one wants to talk to a corporate wall. No one wants to send a text message just to get a reply that says, Hey, our support hours are nine to five Monday through Friday, please email us info@company.com. That's just not a good customer experience. And so what I like to look at is customer experience and how through conversational commerce, how can I build relationships with these humans who are buying from our brand, these humans who are enthusiasts of our brand? (09:13): So what this looks like is texting our customers. I send in our SMS text messaging Welcome Flow. There's a text that people get that says like, Hey, I'm Erica. I'm the person you text, you're texting. I'm the person sending you the text. When you get a text, it's from me. And when you reply, you get a reply from me. So you're not talking to a bot. There's a real human here or an email. I'll send a plain text email asking them about something and I sign it from me and a picture of me so that people can really see like, oh, that's Erica. And it just a encourages them to reply to reach out for help if they need it. But it's also just a fun way for them to share their blanket collection or a picture of their dog snuggled up on the blanket. It's a way to just really connect with our customers.", "token_estimate": 434, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons"], "start_timestamp": "08:05", "end_timestamp": "08:05", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "df3a1005-43a0-4841-abe0-5355ed274204", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:10): So this term has come around in the last six to 12 months, and you mentioned it's kind of coming on the back or it's become popular on the back of SMS marketing. Yeah. I think you described exactly what SMS marketing is, but I'd love if you could give us, I don't want to say the history that sounds like it could be really long-winded, but what is in the history of SMS marketing, where has it been and where has it come recently in the last year or so?", "token_estimate": 120, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:10", "end_timestamp": "10:10", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "9ac54bf0-ab27-41f2-8365-abdbd0808e9d", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Erica Aarons (10:38): Absolutely. So I mean, modern day SMS text message marketing is really new. I mean, there's, sure, we've been all getting texts from politicians for years, but as it comes down to brands that really only started less than two years ago, so it's a super insanely new fields. And the cool thing about SMS marketing is that it's been introduced with all these regulations. So email marketing has, when it came to be, it was just kind of really loose and anyone can send emails and this spam and all that stuff. There wasn't really any regulation behind it. But SMS is really being built that you can't just buy a text list and start sending them emails or sending them text messages. If you text someone who you don't have explicit permission to text, you can get fined like $500 depending on the state you're in. And say you send those messages to 500,000 people and all of a sudden 1% of those comes back reporting, you can actually get in a lot of trouble. (11:55): So one, it's cool because there are actual regulations behind it, but two, a couple fun facts about SMS customers are 97% of customers open a text within three minutes of receiving it. Email open rates are typically around 30% at best, so you're really reaching people really well. And so it's just a cool way to get your message across. And it's also really easy to execute. It's a lot easier to write out 140 character text message campaign than it is to design an email and go through that phase. And lastly, it's just a really easy way for customers to interact with you. It's easy to, in a previous role I had, there's a lot of sizing help you needed help with as a dog product. And so people would send back a picture of their dog and be like, how does this look? Does it look like it fit? And so being able to text a photo versus uploading that to your email and then emailing it and remembering to attach it, it just becomes a really easy channel to connect with your customers. And as a customer support in addition to marketing,\n\nCole Heilborn (13:14): If it's such a great channel. Why has it only been adopted so recently? Why haven't brands been doing this longer?", "token_estimate": 542, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:38", "end_timestamp": "13:14", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "d686e172-aecc-40f2-aea1-31cccc086abd", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Erica Aarons (13:22): Yeah, that's a great question. I think that, I mean, one, there weren't really many brands or there weren't a lot of software tech stacks offering it. First off, I think that is really coming. That's really new. And now there's all these, there are so many different people offering SMS marketing. And so I think one accessibility and it just being new when Facebook was originated, or maybe tiktoks a better example, tiktoks a very new platform and people just started offering TikTok ads, but it just wasn't offered before. I do think that it has been slower. It's been a slower adaption than other means because a lot of times people in charge of the companies are often a lot older and people are like, I don't want people texting me. I don't want to give them my number, so why should we do that? But I think there are so many ways to communicate. Everyone has their preferred method, whether it's a text or a phone call or an email or an actual mail. And so it's really meeting your customers where you're at and not forcing them into SMS or forcing them into email or whatever it may be, but instead giving them the option and be like, if you want to hear from us on text, you can do so. If you want to hear from us on email, you can do so. And so really just giving customers an option.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:56): I see. Yeah. So what are some of the regulations are in place? Because my first assumption is when you say SMS marketing is that now all these companies have my number and now my inbox or my message app is going to be flooded with a whole bunch of texts from people or brands that I have not given my number to. What are the regulations in play and how does that restrict a company's ability to engage with the customer? Or how does it protect the customer, I guess?", "token_estimate": 452, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "13:22", "end_timestamp": "14:56", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "fb802091-6d16-4dc2-baae-f0e23db4a7b8", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Erica Aarons (15:24): Definitely. I mean, it protects the customer and that if a company had your phone number for an order, unless you specifically checked I consent to text messages, you shouldn't be getting text messages. And so it protects you. And just like I know a lot of times it's easy, you buy something online, your emails on them there, and then you start getting marketing messages because the box was pre-checked. That's not allowed on SMS. And so I think one, it's to protect you from getting phone spam because no one wants that. And two, they're like, there are a few different nuances. I know that Florida has a lot of weird regulations about not being able to text customers, or if you text them from 9:00 PM to 7:00 AM that's not allowed. But the, I'd say this SMS providers are really good about, they're the ones with the lawyers researching these, whatever, all these regulations, and they're there to build their platform so that you can't send too many text messages in a 24 hour period, or you can just not send to Florida. You can be exclude Florida subscribers. So I don't even have to worry about that. Yeah. So I think that the nuances and the specific regulations, A, they're there to protect us from getting spam, but B, the software providers are really good about just not letting you fall or they're there to protect you and to make it so that you don't have to worry about all these things when you're sending texts.\n\nCole Heilborn (17:20): Gotcha. So if I was engaging with rumpel, I'm curious how I would end up texting with the person behind the scenes who happens to be you, what does that flow look like and where are places where I could opt in to that option?", "token_estimate": 426, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "15:24", "end_timestamp": "17:20", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "822177dd-4ca5-48e1-ab69-74ba46f3ce76", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Erica Aarons (17:38): Definitely. So we have in certain parts of our emails, sometimes it's like text us from help for help with this number, or there's a popup on our website. You can sign up for texts, and so you can opt in there. And so when you opt into for texts, there are some automatic messages that you'll get sent. The first message is like, Hey, we're rumple. This is what we do. The next message might be a few days later, it might be like, Hey, America, I'm the person you're at Rumpel. Lemme know if you need help mixed with a couple other educational pieces in there. And then finally, if 30 days go by and you haven't made a purchase, you might get another text from me that's like, Hey, it's Erica again. Just wanted to see if you have any questions, if there's anything I could help you with. And sometimes people are like, yeah, actually I couldn't decide between these two. And when they reply, it goes to me and I'm able to be like, oh, I really like this one for this and this blanket for that. And so it's cool to see these conversations and sometimes the outreach is, I set it up, but it is automatic, but the replies are me in real time. And it's cool to be able to see people say, oh my gosh, thanks for your help, and watch them go and convert. Just from that conversation,\n\nCole Heilborn (19:03): How many people do you find yourself chatting with a day? I'm just imagining hundreds or thousands of texts going back and forth constantly.", "token_estimate": 367, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "17:38", "end_timestamp": "19:03", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "25a3576d-e56d-4aa9-8334-fb2edc112558", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Erica Aarons (19:11): Yeah, so we have a pretty small, our list is still pretty small. We've really gone for the strategy of less get as many people to sign up and tell people if they get a bigger discount, they'll get a bigger discount if they sign up for text too. But instead, it's like, okay, of our existing customers offering people who have already engaged with our brand the option to opt in just so it's smaller, more intimate customers and less people who are just signing up to get a discount and then unsubscribing because it is typically a lot more expensive than an email. I would say on a daily basis, I'm typically only texting with two or three people, so there's automations that go out and a few people subscribing every day, but a lot of people just get texts and don't reply to them until they need something, or we prompt them after their order is delivered to say, what did you get? And so people reply and tell me and tell me how excited they're and what they're going to use it for. But yeah, we're lucky that at this point it's very small and it's not a huge, huge ask, but then if sometimes they'll send out a campaign and you'll have messages back from 20 people, and so it definitely fluctuates, but we have an awesome CX team as well, so it explodes. I have the support, just support to get those people in a timely manner as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:54): Gotcha. So fairly manageable at this point. Do people believe that it's a real person on the other end, or do they believe that there's a bot named Erica who just sent a photo of herself to communicate that she's not a bot?", "token_estimate": 403, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:11", "end_timestamp": "20:54", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "89b775ae-5118-4fee-82dd-db0b442a8221", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Erica Aarons (21:08): I think that some people may understand that I'm not going into every text message to automatically draft. I think there's some understanding that it's automatic, but then when people reply and they get a real response from me that's very humanly, I sign my name. It's personalized, and I think they really understand that part of it, and it's fun to see them. Sometimes these customers come up in emails or photos they send in whatever, they might come up in our Slack channel. Someone might be like, oh, Sandy sent in this. And I'm like, oh my gosh, I text with Sandy all the time. She's great. And so it's fun to see that come through.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:00): Gotcha. Is SMS marketing, I guess if you had to take a guess, how many companies are currently using SMS marketing and how many do you predict will be using it in a year from now?\n\nErica Aarons (22:17): Yeah, I think that, I mean SMS, it's obviously big in the e-commerce Shopify direct to consumer space, and I would bet that still under 50% of brands would use it, I would argue. And I think that even just looking back at Black Friday last year for example, I think it was really easy to see like, oh my gosh, how many more? Or when I'm online shopping, I see like, oh, wow, so many more people are starting to have these SMS opt-ins. And I predict that it's probably going to double again in a year or so. I think brands will slowly be like, okay, this is happening. This is something that's sticking around. This is something that's obviously working. And consumers are also becoming more used to it, more used to seeing it. Oh, I'm used to people asking for my phone number.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:17): Yeah. I am curious if there's any stats that you're aware of that speak to how customers respond to SMS. Are they excited about it? Do they opt in for it? Are they reserved? Do they not want to give out their phone number? Do you know of anything like that out there?", "token_estimate": 490, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "21:08", "end_timestamp": "23:17", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "c544894c-f2aa-406b-a00a-10eeb4c77732", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Erica Aarons (23:36): Yeah, I mean, I think that it comes down to age a little bit. I think that younger people, or I know that younger people are more likely to give their phone number. People are also more likely to give their phone number if they already trust a brand, which is why the first time you come to our website, you will not get asked for your phone number. And so I think there's that. And on the contrary, older people, boomers, our parents, they're way less likely to, they're like, I'm not giving you my phone number. And that's not to say that I don't see any older demographics texting me, but I think it definitely has a lot to do with, and I don't know numbers off the top of my head, but it has a lot to do with that age. But I do see other interesting statistics. I see the click through rate on a text message versus an email so much higher. It's just customers inboxes are so much more accessible when you use SMS. But yeah, again, I think having trust for the brand and then other age factors are really going to play into if someone's willing to opt in for sms.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:52): Sure. I guess I'm not familiar with the specific data, but I, I've heard that back in the day when email marketing was brand new, the success rate, the open rate of those old emails was through the roof, probably similar to what it is now for SMS. Is there a point where SMS becomes as diluted and the open rate starts to match? Looks more like email today where it's 20 or 30%?", "token_estimate": 373, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:36", "end_timestamp": "24:52", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "52296d45-541c-4dea-a1d6-959b899cc045", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Erica Aarons (25:20): I mean, definitely I think that we see, we'll see it decline a little bit, but also I think with texts, with email, have a natural tendency to just go through delete, delete, delete. But texts, you can't go into your inbox and hold down and select and delete. And so a lot of times you get a text and you immediately want to click in and just to get that notification to go away. And so I'll be really curious to see what happens with it. But I think that the inbox itself is still there. It's still more accessible. We're still going to see more higher click-through rates, but I think people start to become more selective of who they choose opt in, and so perhaps the massive list growth that people are seeing, it'll start to settle down a little bit and they'll have less subscribers, but the people who are really subscribed and engaged will be the ones that stick around.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:27): Gotcha. So maybe we've all learned our lesson a little bit about giving our email out too much and we'd be more selective about giving out our phone numbers and only giving them to the brands that we really want to have them.\n\nErica Aarons (26:40): Yeah, I mean, personally, I love text Mark. I love SMS marketing, but I don't give my people, you go through the welcome popup and you give them your email and then you give them their number. I more often than not, don't give their number, but I have the brands who I really care about and I give them my number, and I enjoy seeing the text come through and all that good stuff.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:08): Gotcha. So we've focused mostly on SMS, which sounds like it's pretty exciting and there's a lot of new opportunities emerging. I'm curious though, you mentioned there's a bunch of other platforms and tools that you engage with on a daily basis. Could you recap what those are? And I guess I'm curious, is there a different goal for each of these platforms that you're communicating on, or is it the same goal and it's just a different platform to meet the customer wherever they're at?", "token_estimate": 516, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "25:20", "end_timestamp": "27:08", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "6727c098-9312-455e-8169-357d0a4861dd", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Erica Aarons (27:39): I think the next biggest platform is obviously email, and we use a program called Klaviyo, and that is, yeah, it's definitely the biggest and the most important channel that I work with on a daily basis. And so that's sending the welcome emails when you sign up on our website for discount or whatever it may be, those are the emails that you get automatically from sending, there are the emails you get after you purchased, and that might be educational. It might be you ordered this blanket and this is how to care for it. And so three days after your order is delivered, you're going to get a little overview over how to care for your product. And so there's educational aspects there, but also of course, just new product launches and that kind of thing, being able to talk to our customers that way. I send just a fun newsletter every couple of weeks that's like, here are the cool things that are adventure and cozy that I saw over the last week. (28:47): There's this movie on Netflix, and I saw, I read this article about where to travel and whatever it may be. And so not all transactional communication. There's a lot of, like I talked about this conversational stuff weaved in after your second purchase. I might send you an email thanking you for coming back and then joining you to join, asking you to join our Facebook group, for example, which is a small community and separate platform. And the purpose of that is different than email purpose of email is communication, both tried transactionally and for launches educational. And the Facebook community is purely conversational. I do not sell in the Facebook community. I do not let know when we relaunched new product, I do not tell them to go buy things. Instead, I share photos in mine, they share photos of theirs and we talk about it. People offer advice. And so it's a purely a community building objective there. It's another way to talk with our customers, to understand our customers, why they have the blanket, how they use the blanket, if they have multiple blankets, how are those used? And so it's purely just about connecting and building that brand personification like I talked about.", "token_estimate": 548, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons"], "start_timestamp": "27:39", "end_timestamp": "27:39", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "3b1f2873-6227-417c-a9ef-120886a44694", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Cole Heilborn (30:20): Gotcha. Very cool. When it comes to the Facebook group, it seems like it's an idea that, or it's a thing that a lot of people have tried. Let's put together a curated Facebook group where only, for example, customers can come in and engage with each other and chat with the company and other customers. I got to imagine though that it's a ton of work having to engage and to support a community and be there as a moderator. How do you manage that? How do you try to put thoughtful time in without burning yourself out? Are there any guidelines that you have that you use to keep the engagement going and keep conversation happening on the group?", "token_estimate": 167, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "30:20", "end_timestamp": "30:20", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "e5e4d909-dab5-4e30-adbc-c43d2dc0db0c", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Erica Aarons (31:06): Definitely. I think that, I mean, Facebook groups are also an underutilized tool and a, because not everyone has customers who are on Facebook, but B, yeah, it does require moderation, but I've found that once you grow, it's hard to, at first to grow it. We sent emails to our top customers, inviting them in. We've done Instagram pushes where we invite people to join it. And so the first part is part is just building it and getting people to start engaging right off the bat. It's asking them questions about where they took their rumple this weekend, or do you have a pet who steals your rumple or thought provoking or photo provoking questions. But as it starts to grow, it's really fascinating because if people within the community start to want to share their own content, and so I've found that it's really easy to, as you get down the line, people are kind of running the group for you. (32:18): Facebook groups are more likely to show up in people's timelines because it's conversational, it's more natural versus a brand Facebook posts that just probably gets four likes. And so as time goes on, I've really seen that people are excited to engage. And there's still sometimes a couple times a week or a month, depending on what stage you're in that you have to still ask questions or just go in there and talk to people. But it really doesn't take much more than a half hour of my week to go in there and talk to people. And you also have so many, if you seed it and start it with experts of people who are experts on your brand and on your gear, and they're like, someone was asking about our travel blanket. They're like, has anyone had success clipping this blanket to their backpack instead of putting it in? And everyone's like, oh, yeah, it's totally fine. I've done it all the time. And so it's cool to see people advocate for your brand without even having to, so if you're out of the group for three days or a week out of office, people really do a great job of managing that themselves.", "token_estimate": 508, "speakers": ["Erica Aarons"], "start_timestamp": "31:06", "end_timestamp": "31:06", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "c603b776-d326-49d0-9166-57497f4920b0", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Cole Heilborn (33:32): And all of this, to put a bow on it, is this idea of conversational commerce. And it really just comes down to being present, having engaging human conversations with your customers and potential customers. And I guess it's not really limited, sounds like it's not limited to a particular channel, it's more of a mindset to marketing.\n\nErica Aarons (33:54): Yeah, absolutely. I think you can really incorporate it on any channel that you choose, for the most part, especially these own channels. And it's just thinking about your messaging and understanding that your customers, they might love your brand, but they don't want to be sold to five days a week. They want to be talked to, they want to connect with you, and they're excited about the adventures that they go on with your product, and they want to share that with you. And so the more you give them the opportunity and the channels to share that, you start to see more user generated content. You see these people start to get fomo and they see someone else had that blanket and like, oh my gosh, I want that blanket. And so it's really a positive snowball effect. And I think it can be hard for some brands at first like, okay, well, I send this text on a Friday that just asks people what they're doing this weekend. And obviously very few people are going to buy from that. There's no link in it. There's no call to action. It's just like, Hey, let me know what you're doing this weekend. But building those relationships is retention long play. It makes those customers more loyal down the line when they're thinking about getting a Christmas grif for someone or adding another blanket. They're going to say, blanket Rumble. I love Rumble. They're going to recommend it to friends and family. So this snowball effect there is really amazing.\n\nCole Heilborn (35:27): Sure. Awesome. Is there anything we haven't covered that you want to leave our audience with?\n\nErica Aarons (35:36): Great question. I think that, yeah, you can acquire customers all day long, but if you really want to grow your company, think about how you're serving your existing customers too.", "token_estimate": 538, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Erica Aarons"], "start_timestamp": "33:32", "end_timestamp": "35:36", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "f83dba54-be80-42b5-8e9d-1c96f3fedfa6", "episode_id": "6af73b9b-5f90-425a-9354-f6bac217db41", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4892", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Cole Heilborn (35:51): Yeah. Well said. Erica. If folks want to learn more about conversational commerce, they want to learn more about SMS marketing, are there any resources that people can go to learn more about? Is there a place where they can connect with you?\n\nErica Aarons (36:04): Yeah, I can be found wherever social media channel you want by searching my name. I'm really involved in Twitter with lots of other SMS and email professionals, so find me on there if you want to talk and have questions. But otherwise, feel free to look at the people in your network and ask some questions. All these brands also, or these service providers have amazing resources. And I think watching YouTube videos and going to webinars and just reading blogs and staying up has been, staying up on the news has been the most important thing for me in developing my career that way.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:46): Awesome. Well, Erica, I appreciate you sharing, and I'm excited to see where conversational commerce goes in the future. It sounds like it's a great thing. It sounds like it has a great motive, a great heart behind the mindset, and who knows, there could be some pretty neat ideas or technologies that come out of this in the future. So I appreciate you sharing.\n\nErica Aarons (37:10): Yeah, thanks for having me and for allowing me to share the future.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:16): Awesome. Well, I hope you have a great rest of your day.\n\nErica Aarons (37:18): You as well. Bye-bye.", "token_estimate": 370, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Erica Aarons"], "start_timestamp": "35:51", "end_timestamp": "37:18", "podcast_index": 105, "episode_name": "Ep. 105: How Company-to-Customer Communication Delivers Value | Erica Aarons | Rumpl", "slug": "ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-105-how-company-to-customer-communication-delivers-value-erica-aarons-rumpl", "guest_name": "Erica Aarons", "guest_title": "Retention and Engagement Manager at Rumpl", "categories": [], "short_description": "Erica Aarons grew up in MN but has been in the Jackson Hole area for almost 7 years! She worked formerly at a creative agency in Jackson, doing social for mostly Travel & Tourism clients, including Visit Jackson Hole.", "podcast_length": "37:20"}, {"id": "7624cb35-f71b-4fd8-9023-ebfbccf86000", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Marc Gutman (00:03): Coming out of that, I was really excited and passionate about what I was calling a creative manifesto of sorts, which is like, this is how I work, and it's sort of a non-negotiable. These are the things that in order to work with me, I need X, Y, and Z. And some of the things are, Hey, we're going to use our process. We're not going to bend on some of these other things because that's how we can be successful.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Holborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo. Today I'm sitting down with Mark Guttman. He's the founder and chief strategist over at Wild Story Mark. How are you,\n\nMarc Gutman (01:15): Cole? I am great. Thank you so much for having me. Really excited to be here.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:19): Yeah, likewise. People can't see this. I can see it however, on our video call. You've got a microphone in your frame, and I also know that you produce your own podcast, so this format must not be too, must be pretty familiar for you.\n\nMarc Gutman (01:34): Yeah, but it's always interesting being on the other side of the mic and not asking all the questions, but having to answer the questions. So I like both and really excited to see what we talk about today.", "token_estimate": 451, "speakers": ["Marc Gutman", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:03", "end_timestamp": "01:34", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "8d372b90-8c05-4707-af7f-4803e4d18cee", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:45): Yeah, absolutely. Well, I'm stoked you could join us. I know you're a busy guy, you've got a lot going on, but we've got a fun conversation slated for today. We're going to dive into the topic of creating a manifesto for yourself as a creative. And Mark, you've got a really great story and we're going to kind of dive into that and explain what that means. But before we do so, I'd love if you could give us a quick little background on yourself and a little bit of a background on Wild Story, what you do.", "token_estimate": 133, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:45", "end_timestamp": "01:45", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "603b28a4-cd63-451e-9ddf-ed8ff99d911b", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Marc Gutman (02:14): Yeah, thanks, Cole. Well, it's a long story as most people's are right, we got to pick the moments that matter. But I got my start in an area that's probably near and dear to you. I wanted to be in the movie business. I love movies my whole life, and it's the way I kind of saw the world. I grew up in Detroit, so nothing really ever happened in Detroit. So my whole basis for reality was what happened in the movies and of course, where was every movie ever filmed, more or less in California. So when I left college, I went to California. I worked in the movie business for a little bit, and I was really, really fortunate. I worked my way into the business, was able to work with a really well-known academy award-winning director by the name of Oliver Stone. I was his story editor. (03:00): I mean what that meant at the time, I don't know, I just kept getting yelled at and I had to make a lot of copies, but I also was able to oversee the story department, the internship department, and I just had to read a lot. The biggest moment sort of in my career, and I still have it, the biggest trophy that I have is when they presented me with an LL Bean tote bag with the name of the production company on it. And that was so that I could carry 20 physical scripts back and forth from the office every night and read 'em and sit in my apartment and just read till I fell asleep and then started all over again. So that was just an amazing experience crash course in storytelling and telling compelling stories and vetting ideas. And then I had a really amazing opportunity to work on some films at Warner Brothers and 20th Century Fox. (03:50): Again, just worked on animation and animation movie from a while back called Osmosis Jones. And that was an incredible experience because the movie lived on the wall for so long. It was like, here's the scene, here are the storyboards. And they just kept refining, refining and refining and kind of what I learned there. Sometimes that's a great thing and sometimes that can be a bad thing to keep kind of whittling away at the movie movie. But ultimately, I also had this really incredible intense desire and passion for the outdoors. I'd always envisioned that I'd lived somewhere out west. And being from Michigan and why I loved California, I saw after about seven or eight years that my long-term plans in Los Angeles were not there. And so I moved to Colorado and while I was writing, I was writing a spinoff of Osmosis Jones for Warner Brothers. (04:40): I was writing cartoon episodes and I picked up a job at this tech company in Boulder, Colorado, and it was kind of a predecessor to Zoom. It was a communication collaboration company. They were doing a lot of stuff with mobile phones, and I saw this opportunity, so I spun out of there. I was just super enamored with the startup scene in Colorado. It was kind of booming. It was kind of stereotypical what you might see on the Simpsons or something. It was a little bit of a joke, everybody wearing t-shirts with the name of the company on it and stuff like that. But it was also really, really exciting and a lot of things were happening. And so I started my own company and I was doing that, and at the same time, everyone kept saying, well, you worked in the movie business and you're good at marketing your own company. (05:27): We help us with our story. And I was like, yeah, I can do that. And then we would work together and it would end up in a horrible, awful experiment and nobody was happy. And that was because no one really knew what this word tell our story meant. And it means even to this day, it means so many different things. People say it and they think it means, will you help me with my social media? Will you do a brand film for me? Will you help me with my overall brand? We don't what to say. We have a bad sales pitch. Our value props off. And so it took me a while. I didn't know it that many either, and a lot of failed experimentation, a lot of unhappy people. But I just kept at it because I knew and I believe, and I still believe in my heart that storytelling is the greatest thing that we can bring to a business. (06:14): It's the greatest value that we can bring in communicating and adding value to a business. And so over the years, I just kept working at it and I found that, wait a second, what I really think I'm doing is this branding stuff, this brand strategy stuff and setting up a brand story so perhaps people like you can go and tell that story and have a sense of what that might look like or something like that. And so I just immersed in that and I figured that out and taught myself that. And as you know, having your own business, it's like sometimes the best teacher is hard knocks and real world experience. And I had some failures, but more successes and I finally got better and better at it. And here we are today with Wild Story, which is a brand strategy and design studio. And I like to say that we're here to help brands outmaneuver their competition, but we do that through storytelling. (07:06): And what does outmaneuver your competition mean? It just means I want you to stand out. We want you to be different. We want people to connect and resonate with you so that they choose you. I just really believe that in most instances, people aren't really buying. They're choosing. They've already decided if I want to go buy a mountain bike, I know I want a mountain bike right now. It becomes like I got to go find the one that fits me, and it kind of tells the story and it makes me feel like a cool mountain biker and I'm going to pick that one or whatever. So not just a silly example, but I really do believe that we're more choosing than buying and we really want to help companies do that. So that's what we do here. That's the sort of short, long version. That was a little long of a story, but yeah, we could talk more about it if you'd like. But that's what we're doing to help businesses. And a lot of the businesses we work with are outdoor lifestyle, but we also work with B2B and things like that. We just don't publicize it as much.", "token_estimate": 1535, "speakers": ["Marc Gutman"], "start_timestamp": "02:14", "end_timestamp": "02:14", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "b65cc07d-51fd-48fa-9163-0a4805824907", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (08:05): Awesome. And where are you located?\n\nMarc Gutman (08:07): Boulder, Colorado. So big outdoor kind of hub. And we can talk a lot about my feelings around this, but by default, my own personal network happens to be sort of in this outdoor lifestyle space. And then of course, we're in this really big sort of outdoor lifestyle community, so that's why we end up there.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:29): Totally. So it sounds like storytelling has taken different shapes over the years, but it's always been something that's been near and dear to your heart. Where does your passion for story and telling a good story come from?\n\nMarc Gutman (08:45): I think a lot, not a lot, but a couple different areas. I kind of alluded to it, but I grew up in Detroit, Michigan. I had a really great childhood, but it was just kind of a boring, everything else always seemed way cooler than Michigan and seemed way more interesting. And it just seemed like something was happening from a very young age. And I credit probably my mother for this. I was just a voracious reader and I would just transport to different places and I had a really ability to have a vivid imagination. But even I remember I used to sit and just read Thrasher magazine and just imagine what those kids in California were like, and I could really kind of feel the sunshine coming off the page. Even then a magazine like Thrasher, it was really sort of this mixed media. It was photography, it was words, it was kind of like street art and stuff like that. (09:45): So when I think of storytelling, I've always been really drawn to these ways of mixed media and composites. I do think that all our senses really are alive. And then the other thing is my dad and I, we just used to love to watch movies. I think that's how we used to really connect. We had a shorthand and he loved movies, and so we'd watch a ton of movies and just laugh together. And I just always loved that world better than the real world, I think. And sometimes that's a superpower and sometimes that's a weakness, that's a kryptonite to live in that world, but I think that's where that comes from.", "token_estimate": 536, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Marc Gutman"], "start_timestamp": "08:05", "end_timestamp": "08:45", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "5567a730-9861-4a21-9d1a-469178bd33a2", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:26): Yeah. Well, I don't blame you. There's some amazing stories that if we could all immerse ourselves, then it sounds like a great reality to exist in.\n\nMarc Gutman (10:34): Yeah, I always love the story better than the reality, right? It's\n\nCole Heilborn (10:37): Easy go. Yeah, exactly.\n\nMarc Gutman (10:38): Even when you start a business like ah, be everything of the bad things, you're like, this is going to be amazing. This is going to be, and then you think of the idealized and that's always a little bit more fun.\n\nCole Heilborn (10:50): Well, I'd love if you could explain to our audience in your own mind, what is a creative manifesto?", "token_estimate": 167, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Marc Gutman"], "start_timestamp": "10:26", "end_timestamp": "10:50", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "d6c60432-d759-4474-899b-1f93db2c0bd3", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Marc Gutman (10:57): And this is something that we just were talking about, but we work a lot with manifestos manifesto for a business for a person, it's just a rallying cry. It's like what you're about. It's a narrative that you can read and it just embodies what you stand for, who you are, what you believe in. We have somewhere on the internet, it's our most active lead magnet kind of thing, and we have How to Write your own manifesto. And I did a webinar and I talk about it and there's five or six, seven areas that you can just kind of plunk in there. And it's like, who are you? Who are you for? What do you believe? What are your values? Things like that. But then you put it into a narrative and businesses and brands have been using these for 30, 40 years, and it's like, what do you believe? (11:49): It's a rallying cry because even we always talk about values and beliefs, but everyone just kind of throws 'em up on the wall and they're like integrity, right? Everyone's like, what's integrity with a mountain picture? So it really becomes this way that you can get excited, but in its purest form, it's just like what you stand for and what you believe. And so you and I were talking about something that happened to me a few weeks ago, and the short version of that is, so we do brand strategy, but we've been working with a lot of other agencies as well, and they come and they say, Hey, will you work with us? And we're like, sure. And they're like, well, but we have our own process. We have our own format. And going into that, my line was sort of like, Hey, well no problem. (12:42): A lot of things, brand strategies like chicken noodle soup, it's like, hey, everyone has their own sort of special sauce, but at the end of the day, it's basically the same ingredients and the output's basically the same. The only thing really kind of adjusting that output are some of the inputs, which might be budget, which might be time, things like that, size of client. So I went through that process and found that I really struggled and I really had some challenges, and I kind of underestimated how easy it would be to tweak my process into their process. And so coming out of that, I was really excited and passionate about what I was calling a creative manifesto of sorts, which is like, this is how I work, and it's these are the things that in order to work with me, I need X, Y, and Z. (13:38): And some of the things are, Hey, we are going to use our process. We're not going to bend on some of these other things because that's how we can be successful. And what happened as well is, and I think anyone who's in the creative space or creating anything in the marketing space can attest to is it was extremely stressful. It was really hard. It was trying to work something that I wasn't familiar with into an output that wasn't familiar. And I think the only way that these businesses really work for us is when we're able to maintain a process and work the familiar. So that was the idea. That's what I was working on. I had written some bullet points, some of which I've just shared here. I've yet to actually write the manifesto. I've actually yet to put that narrative down because just some things got in the way since we've last talked, but it's on my list.", "token_estimate": 822, "speakers": ["Marc Gutman"], "start_timestamp": "10:57", "end_timestamp": "10:57", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "f8aae87f-6a6a-4eed-abe1-7bb041d6fca2", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (14:36): Were you aware of how important your own process was before this example where you had to adhere to someone else's process?\n\nMarc Gutman (14:47): And this is kind of an interesting nuancey thing, right? I totally abandoned my process. It wasn't like I didn't know my process, but I just thought that the mile markers of my process were enough to hold it all together. And what I realized, and that was a tough thing to learn, was no, that even those little sort of things in the process from output to how you're going to present something to how you're going to go about something really do matter. And so it's not that easy to just plug and play different pieces and things like that. So it was a good lesson to learn.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:32): So let's take a step back before we dive in a little deeper. Why is process so important for creatives?", "token_estimate": 218, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Marc Gutman"], "start_timestamp": "14:36", "end_timestamp": "15:32", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "328a8b11-e45b-4cf9-92c4-d1899ece178a", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Marc Gutman (15:41): Well, I mean, I can only give you my thoughts on this, but a couple things. I mean, I think that anytime that we're given parameters, anytime we're given rules, anytime we're given a box that actually sets us free as creatives, I think the hardest thing to do is to have no guidelines and just a blank page. And you're just like, ah. Right. And also at the end of the day, we're here to please clients. I mean, this is not wild story.org. I know your business isn't a non-for-profit. We're in business and we do need to please clients. And so having some constraint that breeds creativity. So I believe that I also, from my own personal experience, creating on a regular basis is really, really, really hard. And so that process gives you steps and steps in the literal word, like stair steps. It gives you the ability to walk up steps one at a time, even when you're not feeling like it. (16:47): I mean, I don't know about you, but I've had so many times where I've just been like, I don't feel creative. I don't feel like doing stuff today actually. And we just mentioned I came off running a marathon over the weekend. I'm kind of feeling down and it's really hard to work today. I need something that I follow step by step to get me going. It also allows you, it allows me in the sales process, I can tell people what to expect. I know what to expect. It helps to control scope of work. And it also helps once in the sales process, I've communicated to a client, my process acts as breadcrumbs and they know exactly where they are in this process, how long it's going to be, where are we, and we can always go back to that and point to that. And then lastly, I think if you want to, and I use this word very, very loosely scale because it's really hard to scale in the creative space, but if you want to add teammates, if you want people to be involved in your business, having a documented process allows them to keep things going and to keep things consistent, and you can bring people on to help you. (18:04): And I guess on that last thing too, you become more efficient as well. So in the story that I just told, I was extremely inefficient, which caused a lot of stress. I was probably, if I look back and I look at the utilization of my time on that project, not good. I probably lost money or didn't make a lot of money, and I was super stressed out. And so we want to avoid all those things as a creative, and that's what for me at least, process does.", "token_estimate": 622, "speakers": ["Marc Gutman"], "start_timestamp": "15:41", "end_timestamp": "15:41", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "4bade9aa-0202-4e07-94a9-a36615cb018d", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (18:37): This is kind of a sidebar, but I'm curious if you ever forget your process, and this happens to me frequently, we'll be in the middle of a project, everything's up in the air, we're in the middle of the messy creative stuff, and for whatever reason, I lose sight of what is the next step or what are we working towards? And that's often in that moment there's this sense of, oh, shoot, what are we trying to create? What's our next deliverable? What's our next milestone? And then sometimes I have to remind myself and actually go look at my own documentation and say, oh, that's right. I'm working towards a brief or I'm working on a treatment right now. And then that realigns me and recenters me as I'm working on whatever this thing is.\n\nMarc Gutman (19:22): Yeah, I mean, I don't know if we typically lose sight of the process mid project, but what I will say is I'm a constant process tinkerer and collector, and I'm always like, is someone doing it better? And so I'm always trying to change it up or introduce a new process. And then a lot of times it doesn't go well. And I'm like, why did I do that? Everything's fine. I just can't leave good enough alone. And I think part of that too is when you're working on a creative project, I mean, the way that as creatives, we have fun with these is we explore things. We go off on tangents, we kind of push into different areas and it's like part of our personality, but then that's when we get in trouble when you're like, oh, where am I in this project? Or why? And I start tinkering with the process. I've been trying and trying just to hold our process, but I'm always like, ah, I like the way that person does it better, or I've seen this in a different way, but the steps are typically the same. It's just how we get there that I guess I'm always tinkering with.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:37): What I'm curious to understand is your process a little bit and what are the important pieces to it that allow wild story to do what it does? Are there any steps that are super critical to what you do that allow the creative engine to flow smoothly and stay organized?", "token_estimate": 535, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Marc Gutman"], "start_timestamp": "18:37", "end_timestamp": "20:37", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "a6ddbbb5-5bf8-4707-94be-7529f2c74ad6", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Marc Gutman (20:56): Yeah. Well, I mean that's a pretty big question. Absolutely. I mean, everything mean we have sort of operational process, which is just setting up the account in our project management software that we use. We use Notion and getting the shell and everything set up there, making sure that billing's set up appropriately. We have some processes where if you spend a certain amount of money with us as a client, it triggers an automatic welcome gift. So all of those things I think are operational, but super, super important kind of the core things on our process. I'm right now more like I'm putting more and more importance on the front end with the discovery. That used to be something that I think when we started it was just a really quick intake form, and now I'm realizing that everything comes out of that. The challenge is that clients, it's budget. (22:02): And so clients with bigger budget, it's fun because we can take the time to ask questions and really learn and really build. Because what I like to do and what I love about this business in general is I love to come in with a totally empty mind, and I love to come in without expectation and kind of learn. I don't know anything about the industry, even if I do know a little bit. And it takes me down some really interesting paths, and I like to understand how someone might be coming to this for the first time, whatever that might be. And that's going to bring up a lot of questions and hopefully some fresh thinking. And part of that discovery as we get into discovery is I love interviewing people. That's the way I started kind of doing this work, and then I moved away from it. (22:53): It was just so time consuming, but then I was like, you know what? But that's where all the gold is. That's where we really find all the great ideas, all the great insights is interviewing stakeholders, both internally, externally, customers, things like that. So when we think about process, I've been coming way back to, hey, discovery is where it's at. Slowing that down, not budging on that. If a prospect says, look, we've just done this work, or We don't want to pay for that, it is just not going to be a good fit. Now we can rightsize that, of course, buy size a budget, but that's the really, really big thing. And then also we go through values and beliefs. We go through persona, we go through all this brand stuff. And for the most part, we won't sort of gloss over that. If a client's like, yeah, we just did that six months ago. (23:50): I'm like, great, let's pressure test that. We'll bring in their stuff more often than not. Either I'll just use values, core values or something, and beliefs as an example. They're not still valid or they weren't applicable, or we've just learned something deeper and have gone into a new area that we can really build on those and really take them to the next level. And so clients are rarely unhappy after the fact. But I get it, a lot of this work is tough. You're pulling a lot of leadership teams, a lot of important people off their task for a day, two days, weeks sometimes. And so people are really protective of their time. But I just know, and again, I alluded to it in the beginning of when I was telling you in my story, I've had so many failed client interactions that hurt I care. (24:49): I think probably 99% of all creatives are in this business. They love it. They care about their clients, they want to please them. And nothing hurts more than a client that doesn't get what they want or that's upset and they're just not going to get that if we shortcut the process. And so trying to be better about it, but I'm kind of like everybody too. You get this awesome client on the line and they start making all these exceptions and you've always wanted to work with them. And you start to, all right, I'll change it. But it always goes badly. It always goes badly. And then you have that conversation, why did I do that?", "token_estimate": 993, "speakers": ["Marc Gutman"], "start_timestamp": "20:56", "end_timestamp": "20:56", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "231ea3c3-6647-458e-a02d-83cae1e6b7f3", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (25:28): So understanding, I think I have a much better understanding of to you what process is and why that's important. So jumping back to this story, where part of your process changed, what were some of the big takeaways for you in this example and why did that lead you to start thinking about creating your own personal creative manifesto?\n\nMarc Gutman (25:50): I think that much a brand, much like a company and much like a business, we all kind of do these same things, but the thing that's really, really different is the unique flavor we bring to it. And a lot of businesses I spent, and this is an interesting kind of reflection or realization. I mean, this is what I do for my career. I'm like, your special Cole, you're special, and I hope you find what's special and unique about what you bring to video production, but kind of discounted that has merit, that that's something that's really, really powerful and sticky and our process. So our process is probably similar to a lot of other processes. If you look at it on a board, if you were to every little put a Post-it note and then yours and then someone else's, I mean, we borrow things from other agencies from design thinking, but none of it's like, I don't believe I've invented anything new. (26:53): I don't believe that. But the thing that's really, really unique is how we approach it, how we synthesize it. Some of the things can't even be articulated. It's just the way that we see the lens through which we see that particular process and have comfort with it. And so what I learned is that is so powerful, and I think when these other companies are like, ah, can you just change a process or just kind bring it all together into ours? I think I just discounted that. Now the irony or the thing that's really funny is when I did this, the other agency owner, after I presented everything, he's like, oh man, I was really hoping that you would've done your process, that you would've brought your thing. I wanted to see that. That's what I was really excited about working with you on. And I was just trying to make it easy. I was trying to impress this other agency. I was like, if we do well here, there'll be a ton of other work because blah, blah, blah. So I'll just be super cool agreeable client and put it into your process and I'll just make it work easy for you. And then this is just going to be an amazing relationship. (28:09): We have a great relationship. We fought through it. We worked through it. I fought through it, we worked through it, but it was really, really funny when that other agency was like, oh, I wish you would've done your process. And I was like, yeah, me too.", "token_estimate": 672, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Marc Gutman"], "start_timestamp": "25:28", "end_timestamp": "25:50", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "5dabf6bf-f803-4d38-a4cd-187f99fa50f4", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (28:25): Interesting. Did you learn anything about yourself and how you like to work personally through that experience?\n\nMarc Gutman (28:33): Yeah, well look, I got kind of tortured through the experience. I think that the other thing, so I mentioned breadcrumbs for the client. Well, it also gives us breadcrumbs, it gives us clarity on where we're going. And you kind of mentioned that that's a challenge at times for you, but if I don't know where I'm going, I get a little freaked out. I get a little despondent, I get a little stressed out. I'm like, this is not going well. It just feels like sort of this road to nowhere. And so a lot of the work, just to clarify for anyone's listening that we do is the first part is this really sort of heavy lift in brand strategy, but what that output typically is a strong defensible position in the market. And so you might be, for instance, we worked with a Fortune 100 consulting company. (29:33): There's a ton of consulting companies. How are they different? How can they position themselves in a way that resonates with their target market? How can they build a business model around that positioning? It's really big important stuff, and part of that's what makes you different. And we map that out, we figure that out. But sometimes it's a little bit of this, for lack of a better explanation, it's a little bit of this Donald Draper Mad Men pulling sort of this insight out of thin air. Hey, you're this thing or whatever. And so our process helps us to get there. And even then it's a little tough. It's a little stressful. It's like you just don't know. It's never paint by numbers, but when we do different things, then it just becomes like, oh my gosh, are we ever going to even get to where we need to get to? (30:26): Sometimes those positionings, everyone does it a little different. And so then it's like, well, how do we get the output that you want? Everyone does values a little bit different. And the case of the client that I'm referencing, the agency, they just did it different than we did it. I thought they were great. It didn't fit really within their deliverable, and therefore it was just a little bit of a challenge. I think really what people pay us for is our thinking. We need to show that thinking. We need to defend that thinking. And if we don't follow our process, it all kind of becomes patchy and holy and this sort of thinking that doesn't totally make sense because we've been doing it. Someone else is the best way I can describe it.", "token_estimate": 634, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Marc Gutman"], "start_timestamp": "28:25", "end_timestamp": "28:33", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "5b87fc27-bed4-403f-8c86-5c54de98f088", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (31:21): Is there a difference? So we've been using the word process and manifesto kind of interchangeably. Is there a difference between the two? Are they the same thing?\n\nMarc Gutman (31:31): I think they're interrelated for sure, because part of the manifesto, part of a brand is how you do what you do. And so how we do it, how we go about it. But I really do think of process is, but a lot of times too, your process is your approach. It's how we see the world. A lot of times your process embodies your values and your beliefs, and so yeah, they're intertwined. And I think that a really strong process is built and supported by your manifesto. It's built and supported by your brand, and it should all be intertwined because really process too, especially for people in a professional services firm, that is your customer experience. That is your product, that is what people will think about you. Yes, they want the output. Yes, they want the end result, but how you interact with your clients, I mean, that becomes your brand. That becomes what people think of you. That becomes, in a way, your manifesto now. It's just like if you were able to sit on the sidelines and watch you go through an entire process, beginning end with a client, and then you were able to put that into a narrative that would be your manifesto.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:57): So I know you haven't written this down, but it's something that you've been thinking about. If you had to write your own personal creative manifesto, what would be some of the larger components to it? What are some of the critical pieces for you?", "token_estimate": 404, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Marc Gutman"], "start_timestamp": "31:21", "end_timestamp": "32:57", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "d0c15acf-913d-4fd9-b25d-8a4b28a347bb", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Marc Gutman (33:14): I think it's bigger components would be things like who we want to work with. We want to work with forward thinking. Clients that believe in discovery. We want to work with clients that understand that this process of not knowing to knowing that's really the product of what we're selling and that they're patient and that they understand and we want to collaborate. So it's really important to co-create and collaborate. For me personally, I want to collaborate. For me personally, I want to make sure that I have the appropriate amount of support and engagement. A lot of times that comes down to budget because where can we as professional services firms, cut budget for clients, people, our people usually, and we start to pinch there, but yet then that puts more of the load on me personally, and I need the help. I know that I'm at my best when I collaborate with other really smart creatives. And so that's mean. That's one of the biggest ones for me is knowing that. So those are a couple of the things that go into that manifesto of sorts.\n\nCole Heilborn (34:40): It's funny you mentioned the collaboration piece because after when we first connected, we were talking about some of this, and I think you said, I demand collaboration from clients, from team members, whomever. And I walked away from our last conversation thinking about that idea and this idea of what does it mean to be a creative and kind of create your own rules, create your own manifesto for how you want to work. And I don't think I had ever realized, or I'd put it into words, how much I value that collaboration. I think I'd always assumed it, and I always knew I was at my best when I was working with others, but until I actually realized and actually spoke those words or typed them down, that was really powerful because I was like, oh, I know what I need. I need this. And so thinking about process and manifesto and what do you need as a creative, I kind of feel like every creative has their own manifesto, whether they've written it down or not, that they know they need. It's kind of like the best practices or the rules of engagement for how you operate.", "token_estimate": 542, "speakers": ["Marc Gutman", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "33:14", "end_timestamp": "34:40", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "72d00f12-1489-493f-a2e9-c56d0201cf3f", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Marc Gutman (35:48): And I think for me personally, there's been a gap. So I know, and I've inherently always known my manifesto, but I've been too afraid to declare it to clients because you're like, I don't want to offend them. I want to get the work. I don't want to tell them how it's going to be. I don't want to tell them where I'm not strong. And part of in my mind, the manifesto is just that. It's like, Hey, this is me, for better or for worse, let's figure out really quick if me and my agency are a good fit, but in a real honest way, in a real, not almost in a really transparent leap of faith and not like, Hey, I'm going to hide some of the warts, or I'm not going to tell you where I'm not strong, or I'm not going to tell you what I'm really, really good at. (36:40): Because I think my tendency, and I don't know if you have the same thing or if you've seen the same thing with other creative agencies, is to be like, yeah, we can do everything great, and we're strong here and we're here to take care of you. And we see the whole landscape. And I don't have any chinks in the marketing armor, but I do. And it's impossible to be great at everything, and I think clients know that. And so while I haven't written it down, I've been very committed to communicating that to clients. And since we've talked, and it's been about a couple of weeks, so this is definitely something that's an experiment, but at least it feels good for me. It feels like I'm not going to be busted or fall down anywhere. And at least it can always be a little bit of, I told you so, right? Hey, I was very clear. This is how we work. But so far that's never come up. But I think a manifesto does that. It kind of sets you free, lets people know, and hopefully people don't engage because of it as well. I mean, if that's the case and if it's polarizing in that way and not super polarizing, I don't want people to not have business, but certainly want anyone that would be a red flag client to be like, we're just not a good fit.\n\nCole Heilborn (38:00): What has that been like to communicate that manifesto to potential clients over the last couple of weeks?", "token_estimate": 539, "speakers": ["Marc Gutman", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "35:48", "end_timestamp": "38:00", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "b2fdd414-05cc-44cc-961d-d41ad04e8816", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Marc Gutman (38:06): Well, it's hilarious, right? Because it's like all these things that we do in our business that feel really scary, that feel like we're putting ourselves out there, that feel like we're risking making a big risk. Clients are just go, okay, they're terrified. And the client's like, great, because a lot of times they don't know any different. A lot of times that's what they want too. And I think that early returns have been really positive. I mean, I'm fortunate, and I think most of the time when we're working in this outdoor lifestyle space, most clients are really cool. And so that's a real benefit, but we're even seeing it more and more. I think there's just this real humanity coming to the workplace, coming to corporate America. And so I'm even seeing it in bigger corporate clients. And quite frankly, they kind of have to employees, people are demanding better treatment, they're demanding more benefits, they're demanding more respect, and I think there's just this general air, and by no means is it perfect. We're not experiencing the most awesome revolution, but there is this undercurrent of humanity, I think that I'm feeling in business. And so it's a lot easier.\n\nCole Heilborn (39:30): Is it comforting knowing that you feel like you can speak your mind and tell clients how you want to run a project?", "token_estimate": 337, "speakers": ["Marc Gutman", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "38:06", "end_timestamp": "39:30", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "7b26aee0-defd-4379-82c0-8565ab9e7db5", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Marc Gutman (39:38): Of course, yes. Yeah, it's very comforting. But really, so part of it is self-serving and selfish, but really it's all about when I do that, I've minimized so many areas where a project can go wrong. And so we've communicated very clearly how we're going to work together because that really is the product, right? The work we do with a client, everyone likes the output, but you spend so much time with clients, you interact, and it's how we spend our days as people. It shouldn't be a bad experience, but we really minimize all those sort of areas where the project can go off the rails, where we can have missed expectations because that gap of expectations of missed, that's really neither side's wrong. Neither side's trying to be bad. Neither side's evil. But when we have bad feelings or felt like the value wasn't there, it's because expectations aren't aligned. (40:43): And so this is just another way to really align those expectations between professional service firm and client. And once we start to even minimize, run my video here, but I'm making a motion of closing that gap of bringing it closer, it just increases the odds of success. And at the end of the day, that's all I really, really want. And when we're successful, when we're on time, we're on budget, the client's happy. That's just like a home run. That shouldn't be an exception. That shouldn't be the exception to the rule. That should be the rule. But a lot of times when we are unclear with how we work or process or manifesto or what we believe, then it starts to get out of balance.\n\nCole Heilborn (41:34): I really like what you said there. Obviously the deliverable is the product, but maybe another thing to consider is that the relationship and how you work with the client is also a product in some form.", "token_estimate": 454, "speakers": ["Marc Gutman", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "39:38", "end_timestamp": "41:34", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "c89a78b5-8aee-4fc2-a575-5aa2a9446ff8", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Marc Gutman (41:48): Well, for sure. And our business is built on repeat business, long lasting clients. And so if we work really hard, if we have good expectations, if we have good process, if we have good shorthanded relationship with the client, just like any other relationship, if we've been honest and transparent about who we are, how we work, where we've made mistakes, they'll also give us a lot of grace in terms of coming back and allowing us to fix that mistake or to realign on the next project or something like that. So it's just good business for us, but it also, it's just good human interaction.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:34): So distill it all down. What's the takeaway?\n\nMarc Gutman (42:40): Distill it all down. The takeaway is be clear on who you are and who you are not, and then clearly articulate that upfront, loud, and proud to your team, to clients, to vendors. Don't be shy, don't be embarrassed. Don't be scared of who you are. Good things and bad things. Don't be afraid to tell people where you really need help. When I say people, I mean clients like, Hey, this is where we can be really successful and this is where we tend to have problems, or we need to have this component in our process for you. This isn't like a, we're not padding the budget, we're not adding a second camera because we think it's cool. We need that and here's why and just be ready to defend it. But that's the takeaway.\n\nCole Heilborn (43:30): If folks want to go out and take their ideas and put them into their own manifesto of sorts. Do you have any guidelines or suggestions or first steps for people who are trying to figure out how to put all this onto paper and organize it for themselves?", "token_estimate": 425, "speakers": ["Marc Gutman", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "41:48", "end_timestamp": "43:30", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "e00f6c3c-36d4-4e84-964e-b5efafccbf58", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Marc Gutman (43:48): I do, and I'm going to look really quick on the web. I have a whole manifesto builder that I can get to you. We have one page where people can get resources. It's just@wildstory.com slash good stuff. If they want the good stuff, they can go there and learn more about us, kind of follow me on Instagram, have a lot of stuff there where I talk about manifestos, things like that. But we can make sure to put in the show notes. We actually have a full webinar and cheat sheet, kind of a guide that's like in 10 minutes you can lay out the most important things in your manifesto and start thinking about it. But they are the things that are not that are our brand pillars. So things like, who are you for? What do you believe in, what do you do? How do you do it? (44:44): Why do you do it? What's your backstory and what's your vision for the future world? And so those are some areas where you can just go and literally you don't have to write anything grand. You can just do really quick answers, really cool footnotes, and then think about that. The other thing that I talk about in this little manifesto builder package is just go with your phone, put on voice recorder and just go for a walk and just start riffing on these ideas. Transcribe it with a free transcription programmer or something like I use Otter for a couple bucks a month, and you can go and highlight the things that really stood out on your walk and cobble together a manifesto. Super, super easy. And then lastly, you can also employ your team copywriters, things like that. You can really go for it and really think about it. But at its base, it really is about how you feel from the heart and what you believe in. And so smaller business, you have all the answers. If you're a larger business, sometimes you have to involve the team and kind of get some other inputs, but a lot of these things, the process is the same whether you're a bootstrap startup or whether you're a Fortune 100 company.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:03): Yeah, well said. And the backlink for that resource was what? Again?", "token_estimate": 521, "speakers": ["Marc Gutman", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "43:48", "end_timestamp": "46:03", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "dbbbd93c-efa9-4d9e-84ad-9de1449fe704", "episode_id": "0398ef63-2f40-4be8-b7bf-87771185455c", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4893", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Marc Gutman (46:08): I'm going to find it and I'll make sure that you have it for your show notes, and so you can list that with the show notes.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:17): Okay. Appreciate it. Is there anything that we haven't covered in our chat today that you want to leave the audience with?\n\nMarc Gutman (46:23): I was about to say, there's so much we haven't covered, but anything that I want to leave the audience with? No, I think that was great. I think we talked about a lot. We talked about this fresh idea of needing a creative manifesto, but really it's like being really clear on who you are and where you can be successful, and you'll just be much happier in all your professional service endeavors.\n\nCole Heilborn (46:47): Yeah. Well, mark, if folks want to follow along with you, if they want to follow along with Wild Story, where can they go to connect?\n\nMarc Gutman (46:53): Yeah, thanks, Cole. Really easy. They can go to wild story.com. That's our main website. As I mentioned. If you go to wild story.com/good stuff, that's where you get the good stuff. I have all sorts of links there where you can connect with me on social. You can take some of our free brand health audits and things like that. Just a lot of good resources to get started. That's your first step on the brand journey. And then if anyone wants to get in touch with me, feel free just to shoot me an email@markwildstory.com to reference this podcast, and we'll be happy to help you out.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:30): Awesome. Well, mark, I appreciate you taking the time to chat. This was a lot of fun, and I wish you the best on your next marathon, whenever that might be.\n\nMarc Gutman (47:38): We'll see. Like I said, I just got back yesterday. I'm barely able to walk, but hopefully it'll be soon. It'll definitely be more in my future. Thank you so much for having me on the Back Country Marketing Podcast and just really enjoyed it.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:49): Yeah, thank you.\n\nMarc Gutman (47:50): Yeah, thanks, Colt.\n\nCole Heilborn (47:51): Take care. Thank you for listening to this episode of the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. If you enjoyed today's episode or learned something new, please consider leaving us a review on Apple or sharing this with a friend.", "token_estimate": 562, "speakers": ["Marc Gutman", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "46:08", "end_timestamp": "47:51", "podcast_index": 106, "episode_name": "Ep. 106: Building a Creative Manifesto | Marc Gutman | Wildstory", "slug": "ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-106-building-a-creative-manifesto-marc-gutman-wildstory", "guest_name": "Marc Gutman", "guest_title": "Owner at Wildstory", "categories": ["creative"], "short_description": "Marc Gutman is an award-winning storyteller, entrepreneur, adventurer, and idealist. But most importantly, Marc loves brands and their stories. He is the founder of Wildstory, a storytelling agency dedicated to helping visionary entrepreneurs and businesses get crazy clarity on their story.", "podcast_length": "48:13"}, {"id": "8b7353f2-51dd-4dc1-bfa3-1c8acc24d380", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (00:03): Internal and external brand. I actually, personally, and I think we're seeing more and more organizations aligned for this, is that they are not mutually exclusive. You have a brand and it needs to fulfill the expectations of the customer or the consumers of your products just as much as it needs to fulfill the expectations of the people that work for you, your staff, your employees.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:33): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing podcast. My name is Cole Hallborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Back Country Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo, and today I'm sitting down with Julia Vecchio. She's the director of marketing at CMH Heli Skiing and Summer Adventures. Julia, how are you?\n\nJulia LoVecchio (01:17): Pretty good, thanks, Cole. How are you?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:19): I'm doing wonderful. Excited to be sitting down with you this morning and looking forward to our conversation.\n\nJulia LoVecchio (01:26): Me too.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:27): So where are you calling in from?\n\nJulia LoVecchio (01:31): I am currently sitting at our office in Banff National Park in Alberta, Canada, and this is probably my seventh time to the office in two years.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:45): Oh, well, it sounds like it's been a busy day so far, a lot of strategy meetings. Is that what brought you into the office?\n\nJulia LoVecchio (01:50): Absolutely, yeah. But I love being here. I'm reminded of how much I actually love the environment that I work in for sure. Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:59): So on the tail end of the ski season, is this when you guys refresh and reset for the summer part of your business and start planning for next winter?", "token_estimate": 540, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "00:03", "end_timestamp": "01:59", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "c92fb098-18ab-40b2-91f7-dda43a07908f", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (02:10): I mean, I would say we actually typically this time of year, we've already sold through a significant portion of our winter offering. This year we did things a little bit differently, so we are actually smack dab in the middle of what we call our booking week where we release our dates and prices for the following season. Normally we would've done that back in October, but just due to the world we shifted it to this year. So yeah, we are very focused on getting our summer operation up and running the three lodges that we operate and really on focus more on the hiring side for that. And then yeah, we are looking like we're going to be very full next year. I think we might've broken the phone lines on Tuesday morning when we things went up for sale. So yeah, I mean it's a great feeling, but it's also a bit insane right now.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:10): So when we first connected, I did not realize this, but you enlightened me that CMH is actually was the first company to offer skiing. Does that mean that you invented heli skiing or what's the origin story?\n\nJulia LoVecchio (03:23): We actually did invent heli skiing. Our first commercial operation was 1965, but previous to that, our founder, Hans Moser had been operating, he immigrated to Canada from Austria and he had been operating ski touring, guided ski touring experiences in the interior British Columbia. And I think the Lord has it that he was ski touring and he saw a plane fly over and he was like, man, there's got to be a way that we can get people into the mountains in an easier way. And he actually contacted a helicopter operator and said, Hey, would you guys be willing to let us hop in and go for a lift? And so in 1965, that's when we first started operating heli skiing in the bugaboos area in the interior British Columbia. And that was our first lodge that we built. I think it was in 67, been going ever since. So\n\nCole Heilborn (04:28): How do you know for sure that that moment was the first heli ski moment?", "token_estimate": 506, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "02:10", "end_timestamp": "04:28", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "2a3fa519-819a-4430-bfd1-635ec1ffd19b", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (04:34): Well, I mean, oral history is what it is. I would say there have been almost no arguments against that. There is some website out there that does claim that that is not true, but I think that it is commonly accepted around the world, the heli catt world, that Hans Moser was the first to invent the sport.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:05): Wow. Well, I'm curious to learn a little bit more about your scale of operations and also there's the whole summer side of your business as well. It's not just wintertime.", "token_estimate": 132, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "04:34", "end_timestamp": "05:05", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "49910e3c-6975-4b73-b7b7-e8ffe853209c", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (05:16): So we operate, we own and operate 12 individual areas. We've traditionally called them lodges. We now call them destinations because in some instances our operations don't have a lodge actually associated with it, doesn't have accommodation. So yeah, 12 areas within the interior of British Columbia. Our head office is here in Banff in Alberta, but all of our operations exist in bc. We run from early December in the wintertime, early December through to the beginning of May. So it's roughly 22 weeks of operation, which is a very long season. Our guests come from all over the world, predominantly United States and a handful of European countries. And then the rest is sort of the Canada and the rest of the world. Australia is a big feeder for us as well. And then in the summertime, which interestingly enough, we've only been operating our summer, so we've been operating since 1965 in the winter. (06:22): Our summer operation is about 30, 30 ish years old. It was created, we had, there's an organization called TA Tours, and Arthur, the founder of TA Tours, he was a very good friend of Hans Mosers, and he kept on pressuring, he was a guest of ours in the winter, and he kept on pressuring Hans saying, man, you've got these incredible lodges. Why aren't you using them in the summertime? And Hans said, oh, no one wants to, no one wants to come and do that. Everybody hikes in Europe on their own. We don't need this. And Arthur kept on saying, trust me, I can fill your lodges with my guests that I bring to the Canadian Rockies to experience this wonderful landscape. And Hans, it took Arthur several years to convince Hans to actually allow us or to allow him to bring guests. And probably for the first 20 years, 15 and 20 years of us actually having a summer operation for heli access hiking, Arthur was really predominantly our only guest. (07:29): He brought the guests for us and Arthur's guests were a little bit older and maybe a little bit more less experienced in walking around in the mountains and the helicopter, similar to what the helicopter provides us in the winter, it provides us access to places and activities that we wouldn't necessarily be able to do without it. Yeah, I mean, I continue to say it's my greatest career challenge is filling our three operations in the summertime. It's bizarre, but it's the equation of you need this helicopter to get to this place that is so easy for us to make as skiers and snowboarders. It's not the same in the summer.", "token_estimate": 630, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio"], "start_timestamp": "05:16", "end_timestamp": "05:16", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "b8db42b7-7b0f-47d5-b934-c7ef0b859f8c", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Cole Heilborn (08:14): I'm curious if mountain biking is something that's ever been questioned or is an offering in the summertime?\n\nJulia LoVecchio (08:22): It's definitely been a question. I think the mountain biking industry is shifting. We are seeing more people travel, spending more of their disposable income on mountain bike experiences. But I would say one of the beauties of our summer experiences right now is there are no trails. When we think about hiking, like traditional hiking, what comes to mind in my little brain is I'm walking in front or behind a group. We're on the single track piece of trail. We're not really talking to each other with the helicopter. What that enables us to do is land somewhere and go for a walk, and we actually do leave a very small, we quote footprint on the landscape helicopter leaves, and we go for a walk and we only have very few people with us. So that, I'd say the intensity of building trails is something that we're not sure at this point that the benefit will be there in the market. There are operators that are doing it, certainly in our world, but yeah, it's not completely off the table, but we're focused on hiking right now and with 80% of the people that go outside to find themselves as a hiker, we think they're still a market for us.\n\nCole Heilborn (09:51): Yeah, interesting. Well, I'm curious, when did you enter the role of director of marketing with the company?\n\nJulia LoVecchio (09:58): March 15th, 2015. Yeah, so seven years. And I often say that I won the career lottery. I think I have one of the best marketing jobs out there. It's been a real honor to work for such a famous and impressive organization.", "token_estimate": 421, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Julia LoVecchio"], "start_timestamp": "08:14", "end_timestamp": "09:58", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "bb2fba75-3305-43f8-a9ac-ea3f19856e66", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:19): So I want to dive into our topic today, and you can kind of, I think set this up for us, but to kind of tee things off, so we've had the opportunity to connect prior to this, and one of the things that you've mentioned was when you stepped into the company seven years ago, there's kind of this inflection point where you had this great past, this great history, but the question was, where are we going in the future? What does the future look like? And this idea of aligning the internal and external brand started to be a question that it sounds like you and the team started to ask yourself. So I'm curious, what are those two, the internal and the external brand, and what was this inflection point that you found yourself in?", "token_estimate": 189, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:19", "end_timestamp": "10:19", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "17d0bcad-546b-477c-b78f-f2ec8f423ec6", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (11:00): When I think about internal and external brand, I actually personally, and I think we're seeing more and more organizations aligned to this, is that they are not mutually exclusive. You have a brand. Your brand is your brand, and it needs to fulfill the expectations of the customer or the consumers of your products just as much as it needs to fulfill the expectations of the people that work for you, your staff, your employees. And it's almost, there's times when I feel like it's similar to people that say, oh, well, I don't actually accept friend requests or allow my social media feeds to be part of the people that I work with because that's my personal life and my work life is not the same. And I actually believe in a modern world, there's this very gray blending that's happening where the things you do every day, the things you get paid to do every day need to be aligned with the things that you sell. And when they're not, it becomes more difficult for everybody. (12:29): So I mean, I really do think that when we talk about for us at CMH, we talk about our mission is that we guide you through life-changing mountain experiences, and that is, and then our vision is to be recognized as the world leader of guided mountain experiences by our guests, our staff, our partners, our vendors, et cetera. And I think by all of us understanding that not only are we providing these mountain life-changing experiences for our guests, that we ourselves have been transformed by the mountains, it is very, very central to who we are as a company.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:26): Yeah. So I'm curious, what challenge did you face when you stepped into the role?", "token_estimate": 421, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "11:00", "end_timestamp": "13:26", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}, {"id": "5c38283c-02ba-472f-b791-2d857db9d774", "episode_id": "84a874ba-197b-437c-86ce-c7c62f7e4899", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4894", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Julia LoVecchio (13:33): There were a couple key challenges from a marketing standpoint that we saw, certainly that I saw. It is probably two to three years prior to my arrival for every year that our company was growing older, as was the average age of our guests. And we recognized that the loyalty based model that we had survived on and built an extremely successful company on while was very successful. Those loyal guests were aging out and we needed to go and find more guests like them or a new set of guests that were able to spend the next 20 years with us. So we needed to make a shift between from a loyalty based marketing organization to an acquisition based marketing organization. And I would say that prior to my arrival, the pendulum swung very, very, very far to the acquisition side. And it needed to be a mind shift that was happening internally as well. (14:48): But we did it in a way that almost spoke to that everything that we were doing in the past was wrong. And now when I came in, I said, okay, we actually can't go out and find all new guests. That's not going to happen. This is a very niche market. What we need to do is we need to find a balance between how we communicate as an organization that pays an enormous amount of respect to where we came, the guests that helped us build this business, the people that we've been hosting for years who very clearly have become our very, very good friends and almost family. But we also need to stay relevant and be relevant for a new group of people that are going to come in and spend time with us. And there is a fine balance between those things, especially in an environment where you are in the back country at a beautiful lodge sitting in a helicopter with a very small group of people, you need to make sure that you're meeting the expectations of those that have come before while also meeting the expectations of a new group. And it was an excellent revelation for us. But how you do it is ensuring that the team that's focused on whether that is acquiring an email or having a connection, an initial brand interaction with our organization, right through to the people that are going to be rebooking, guest X, that has been filling a particular lodge on a particular week for 15 years, that there similar efforts are going into both.", "token_estimate": 581, "speakers": ["Julia LoVecchio"], "start_timestamp": "13:33", "end_timestamp": "13:33", "podcast_index": 107, "episode_name": "Ep. 107: Aligning Internal Brand and External Brand | Julia LoVecchio | CMH Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "slug": "ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-107-aligning-internal-brand-and-external-brand-julia-lovecchio-cmh-heli-skiing-and-summer-adventures", "guest_name": "Julia LoVecchio", "guest_title": "Director of Marketing at CMh Heli-Skiing and Summer Adventures", "categories": [], "short_description": "Julia's career has followed a somewhat unique and non-linear path, and as a result she's had the good fortune to gain leadership experience and build management skills across the tourism, hospitality, and communications industries. One element that has remained central to her journey has been a life rooted in the mountains and mountain culture.", "podcast_length": "53:55"}]
